era Greythorne, the man Marcus Lampson. He was a short, thick-set man of five-and-thirty, He walked back to his studio like a stricken with pallid, heavy features, black moustache, and dull, sleepy eyes. It was said that while he was man. He could not believe that the deception of a woman could have had such an effect upon making his fortune no man was sharper, but that him. -when he had made it no man was duller. The "She must have told me lies from beginning to cause was champagne and brandy. . . . end," he muttered.</p>
        <p>[ALL nICITITS TtrzEllVll\l THE TYO RY BOX.</p>
        <p>It cannot be Said Dick Halford was in tune with the occasion. In the first place, he hated the slightest suspicion of patronage, and there was, to him, something of this in the whole affair. He was half sorry he had accepted, but he was persuaded into it by some of his journalistic comrades. Certainly he said very little during dinner, but this, perhaps, was due to the fact that he was thinking of Vera Greythorne. Suddenly he was roused by the voice of his next- door neighbour, Harry Knight, a popular opera bonfP comedian, saying: "And how is the masterpiece progressing, Halford?" "All it, wants is the finishing touch—a pur- chaser," returned Halford. At that moment he drew his, handkerchief from his pocket. Something rattled on the floOr and rolled to the feet of the actor. It was the gold- inlaid ivory box, the treacherous lid of which coming off the contents were disclosed. A dainty powder puff—that was all. A roar of laughter greeted the discovery, and a little blush of mortification went over the painter's face. "A gage anzour !" exclaimed Vanstone, a dramatist, merrily, from the other side of the table. "She must be madly in love, Halford, to give you so precious a woman's weapon. Lucky dog !' "Don't be an ass, Vanstone," growled Halford. "There is no mystery. A model left it in my studio this afternoon, and I picked it up." The dramatist quietly turned the box over and minutely examined the lid. The eyes of the actor were also fixed upon it. "Would you lika me to describe the owner ? " said the former, laughingly. "I think I could assist," put in the actor. "What do you mean ?" demanded Halford, half fiercely. " • -</p>
        <p>The next morning his depression hadn't worn off. Work was still impossible. He pottered about his studio, he lighted innumerable pipes, some of which he smoked and others he allowed to go out after a few puffs. In a word he was • thoroughly unhinged. Then he had a visitor. She was a stranger, and had called to know if he wanted a model. He glanced at her, and saw the possibilities of a picture of London life. The large, limpid, laughing eyes, the plump, dimpled cheeks, the impudent, assertive somewhat tip-tilted nose, the small, firm mouth, the saucy chin and the fluffy golden hair made a combination quite typical of a certain kind of London girl. Her accent had decidedly a Cockney twang. disposed to interfere itnu it3Stl tn.n ever how tru,f; "Is my face any good for you to paint?" said she had proclaimed she was Vera Greythorne. . she, coming to business at once. A thrill of joy went through his heart. But he ."Oh, I daresay- I can make use of you, was still mystified. rejoined the painter. "Can you give me a "Perhaps you've forgotten you gave me this ? " sitting now ?" went on Miss Greythorne, suddenly flourishim; It occurred to him he would force himself to something she was holding in her left hand. work as a remedy for his melancholy breedings. It was the ivory puff-box. "Of course I can. The sooner I get a little "How it got here I don't know. but I found it cash the better I shall like it. 1 daresay you've on the table, and if there's anything wanted to got some powder. My complexion's simply make you keep your word like is gentleman it horrid to-day." ought to be this I ix. I was that hard up I hal The painter did not reply, for at that moment to pawn it for three quid, and afterwards sold the electric bell announced a visitor. His elderly the ticket to a girl for ten bob. And all attendant was out. Halford himself went to through you, M—M—Mareus. Oh, it was the door. Miss Vernon, as she called hereslf, erooej_crooei - meanwhile discreetly went to