        <p>The dramatist (quietly swept the powder on one side, and handed the box to the artist, bidding him look at the bottom. Halford darted a hasty glance, and saw two sets of initials scratched by the side of the name "Vera Greythorne " engraved in the ivory. The initials stood fOr the names of the dramatist and the actor. "I daresay this is an interesting discovery for you," said the former, slowly. "I confess it is so interesting tot me that I should like to have v little further explanation." "Nothing is easier, old man. That box when last I saw it was in the possession of the original owner, Vera Greythorne. She was the star of Montressor-Smith's operatic company. She was all the rage in Johannesburg when the company was there just before the war. The old tale, you know, of a pretty, spoilt woman fond of admiration, with a strong will,. and a wealthy man with more money than braius. He spent a fortune on her and it's a wonder she didn't marry him, but I fancy he discovered his folly in time. The painter tossed down a glass of wine. Ho felt faint and sick. "And the initials ? " said he, mechanically.</p>
        <p>'a little rough on J.animy. who's nC-it such a "Just a word with you," he burst out, without bad sort as he looks. We've made it tip, and any preface. "Not here—in your studio." the wedding's coining off sharp. You'll have to Dick Halford looked at him perfectly unmoved. come—won't he, Launny ? "Oh, by all means," said he, indifferently. Mr. Lampson scowled, and grunted out en They walked into the studio, and now that ungracious acquiescence, it was clear he hail no:: Halford had more time to look at the millionaire forgiven the painter for coining between him and he saw he was in a white heat of passion. What the other lady, whoever she might be. did it all mean ? So they left. Milford stood for some time in "See here," went on Lampson, in a thick, front of the easel, contemplating his work, and choking voice. "I want to settle the question of then with a sigh turned—to see the original that portrait I offered the other night to buy of in her nurse's costume at the other end of the you. Are you going to sell it or not?" studio. She had just entered. He rushed "If I were disposed to sell the picture, your towards her with radiant face. method of-bargaining is so little removed from "Where have you been ?" be cried. "I bullying I should say no." thought I should never see you again." "I thought that would be your answer," "Forgive me," said she, it little tremulously. replied the other, fiercely. "I came prepared "I'm afraid I've inconvenienced you terribly. '1 with an alternative." do hope the picture hasn't suffered. I was sent He produced from under his arm a mahogany away suddenly on a private ease, and as the lady case, and opened it It contained a brace of was a personal friend I could not refuse. I pistols. ought to have written, but I did not lil7e per "You'll find them ready loaded," he went on, petrating a deeeption,on paper. 1. called myself "and you can take your choice. One is as good Vera Greythorne, as you will remember ; but tliat as the ot