        <p>"Oh! they came about one wild, larky night —the occasion of the hundredth performance of Fleurette.' The libretto of the opera was mine, as I daresay you know. Knight and I scratchrid the letters on the box in her dressing- room." Raiford was silent. He had learnt all he wanted to know, and more. He felt utterly wretched. The coincidence was too striking, but ho _would not pronounce judgment until he had</p>
        <p>Perhaps you'd like to know a little more. I hesitatingly: meant to make the original of that portrait on the "I have to make a confession, :dr. Ilford. I easel Jver yonder Inv wile. I proposed to her did not mean to see you any more, but I lied to last night. She refused me. Of course, the ecrne. 1 did not know what that horrihM man reason is plain. You have forestalled me." might du, and I wanted to %yarn you." His manner was so insulting, and his assertion iq understand," iirni:o in Lit..lft‘ri. was so monstrous, that Halford was infuriated beyond measure. "You lie!" he exclaimed, hotly.</p>
