      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>SOMETHING NEW IN ,TALNING MACHINES.</head>
        <p>SOMETHING NEW IN ,TALNING MACHINES.</p>
        <p>Every user or prospective user of a talking machine (and we may almost say that every music lover is the one or the other now-a-days) will be interested in a few particulars regarding a decided sensation in cylinder talking machines which has been brought out by the Columbia Phrniograph Company. The instrument in question is called the" Crown " GRAPEI-o-phone and there is a special feature introduced into its equipment which gives to its reproduction of vocal or instru- mental music such a beautifully clear, rich, mellow , ! tone, as at once to distinguish the " Crown "from all I other cylinder talking machines. The special feature r consists in a " tons-arm" made of aluminium ingeni- ously connected with the reproducer. It has been I known for some time that aluminium metal possesses the remarkable property, when used as a sound con- veyor, of wholly eliminating all false vibrations land producing a perfectly pure, natural effect. Some few years ago the Columbia Company introduced a I tone-arm of this metal into the disc style of talking I machine, with the result that quite a revolution was effected in the disc trade. It will he seen, therefore, how important is the announcement that, in the "Crown ' GRAPH-o-phone, the same-well-known firm have now succeeded in applying this significant aluminium device to the cylinder style of talking machine. Then, in addition to the wonderful improve- ment in tone, there is a noteworthy advantage in con- ven,ence in the " Crown " model. With the ordinary stye of cylinder instrument, it is necessary to use an unsightly crane or stand and rubbar tube connection if a large horn is to be employed. With the " Crown," however, all extraneous fitments are dispensed with, the build of the instroment enabling a large, tone-enforcing flower horn, which swings in any direction, to be supplied as part of the ordinary equipment. Notwith- standing its unique merits, the " Crown " GRAPH-0- phone is retailed at the moderate price of six guineas. A special descriptive illustrated leaflet is issued by the manufacturers, the Columbia Phonograph Company, General (Dept. 20), 61 and 66, Oxford st., London, W. Every music lover should write for a copy which will be sent post free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>xammer Saturday, March 28, 1908,</head>
        <p>The xammer Saturday, March 28, 1908,</p>
        <p>horrible man hag been here. That's his hancli work. Fortunately, it can be repaired." He pointed to thc bullet hole in her portrait. "Do you mean ii;) really tired a pistol at you? Oh !--"</p>
        <p>She could say no more. Her lips went very white, her eyelids drooped. He was ja Iver id in.a moment, his arm was round her waist.</p>
        <p>"Dearest," he whispered, "I also have a con- fession. I love you, and my cnly regret is I did not tell you so long ago. When you didn't come I thought I had lost you for ever. I've suffered tortures while you were away. Vera--" He stopped and looked at her. He suddenly recollected her name Was not Vera. Her hands were covering her face, but he could see her scarlet cheeks. Whatever her name might be he loved her. He gently pulled her hands aside and kissed her full on the mouth.</p>
        <p>"Oh!" she murmured, tremulously, "you should not have done that. I have deceived you. lam ashamed. I gave you a false name, and it was to tell you that and to explain how I knew Mr. Lampson'that I've come to-day. I saw you that day, Mr. HaHord, when I was riding in the Park, and I felt sure you saw me." "Yes, yes," he interposed, hastily; "but why go into that? Mr. Lampson has been here, and in a fit of insane jealousy accused me of coming between him and the original of yonder portrait. I fancy, however, he is now in good hands —the hands of the owner of a certain ivory box which in some mysterious fashion found its way into my studio bearing the name under which I knew you that is, Vera Greythorne -- a lady who, if I mistake not, means before long to be Mrs. Lampson —all the better for Mr. Lampson, who certainly needs someone to take care of him." "That unfortunate box!" she cried. "The name at the bottom took my fancy, and when you persuaded me to it for you I gave it you instead of my own. The box was given me by a poor girl, a chorus singer, who died in the hospital. It was all she had, and just before she breathed her last she begged me to accept it. That is tho history of the box." "Not entirely. Shall I tell you the rest ?" She listened to him attentively as he told her what had happened at the dinner, and how Marcus Lampson had recognised the box. But for this most certainly the 'millionaire would not have visited the studio, and the painter would not have known how she had refused to marry him. "Did he tell you I refused him ?" "Of course he did. It was his grievance against me." "You ought to know everything," said she after a pause. "My mother did all she could to persuade me to zr arry him. Shn was never tired</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LONDON &amp; NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY.</head>
        <p>LONDON &amp; NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>Inter-District Games Competition.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. GARSTON.</p>
        <p>A most enjoyable evening was spent at the Warring- ton Railway Institute on Thursday evening last, when various Game Matches were played between the staffs of the Warrington and Garston districts. There was a very large attendance of competitors and spectators, and the various matches were keenly followed. The Warrington District staff succeeded in winning three matches out of four, as the under- mentioned details will show. The games were followed by a hot-pot supper, and a yery successful and enjoy- able evening wee thus brought to a close. Amongst otners, the following gentlemen were , present :—Messrs. F. A. Sargent,district goods manager, I Warrington ; S. H. Hunt. district traffic superintendent, Garston; J. Rougley, assistant dock superintendent, Garston ; R. Bolton, station master, Warrington; W. ' Hunt, goods agent, St. Helens; J. Houghton, district superintendent's dept., Garston ; W.H. Thomas, locomo- t tive department, Speke.</p>
      </div>
