      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The End of a Rainy season.</p>
        <p>BRIGHT PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>Though the 1907 season was one of the worst ever experienced from the weather point of view, it did not detract from the success of the Warrington Cycling Club and the organization was never in a healthier con- dition than at present—in fact the membership constitutes a record. Judging from the large attend- ance and the enthusiasm manifested at the annual meeting which was held at the Patten Arms Hotel on Thursday evening, the coming season should be in accordance with the best traditions of the club. Mr. F. Fieldhouse (captain) presided and the gathering included Mr. F. Garnett (hon. treasurer) ,and Mr. G. H. Smith (hon. secretary). THE DOINGS OF THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. H. SMITH said it‘was at once a privilege and pleasure to present the twenty-second annual report of the club—a privilege to record an official connection in the capacity of hon. secretary with a club so well- established and so representative of the sport, a pleasure to chronicle the fact that in the twenty-second year of its existence it was found numerically stronger and at least as vigorous as at any period of its life. The total membership now numbered 114. From a climatic point of view the seascin of 1907 must inevitably be distinguished by the more than average amount of rain and cold winds encountered. The opening of the riding season was dismal indeed and one's imagination went hack to the then recently elected captain, fired with enthusiasm of his new duties, dolefully week after week studying clouds that broke not whilst vainly awaiting the flying squadron which was to be his to command. (Laughter.) For weeks the runs were invariably spoiled by the weather, and May 1907 must surely stand out as one of the worst Mays on record. Still it was remarkable how the members gathered when there was the least chance of a ' spin—possibly in sheer desperation—and it could at least be stated that on no occasion was a severe drench- ing experienced and dodging the wet was reduced to a fine art. On Saturday, July 9th, the members were once more the guests of their esteemed president (Mr. John Naylor) as Beeston Towers. It was indeed a pleasure to recount these outings year after year and the interest in this the twelfth visit to Beeston could truly be said to have lost nothing of its keenness, another link beihg added to a chain of tender and delightful memories. Towards the middle of July the real summer weather commenced and they had an opportunity of basking in the sunshine, of which they had had so little, and the runs were thoroughly enjoyed. On the 24th August the annual fishing match took place at Little Leigh, the victors being : I, J. B. Poole; 2, Andrew Robertson • 3 and 4. F. Garnett and W. A. Ashton. On Thursday August 29th a little varia- tion was made in the shape of a cricket match with the Thursday C.C. It was perhaps hardly neces- sary to say that the cyclists were not successful, but an illustration was given of the versatility of some of the members in matters relative to sport. (Laughter.) The close of the riding season arrived with a run on September 21st when a bowling main was held on the green at Sankey Chapel House and resulted as follows : 1, G. F. Freeman; 2, Joseph Heinen ; 3, G. H. Smith; 4, W. Unsworth. The [Peerage attendance at club runs was on Tuesdays 11•4 and on Saturdays 16 which, when the weather was considered, ooLld be said to be fairly satisfactory. G. Anderton (1), A. Spinks (2) and W. Delmage (Tuesday prize) proved the winners of the attendance prizes. The club was again fortunate in securing the exclusive use on Wednesday evenings of the bowling green at Sankey. Their fellow member, Mr. W. Unsworth, was to be thanked for allowing them the privilege. The principal main was concluded on July 6th, the winners being :—Watkins challenge cup and first prize, W. Unsworth ; 2, El. Watkins ; 3, G.H. Smith; 4, A. Spinks. Third round consolation, 1, G. Anderton ; 2, F. Fieldhouse ; 2nd round consola- tion, 1, G. Bowman; 2, J. W. Bullen ; Ist round con- solation, 1, A. Booth ;2,5. G. Throssell. Two bowling matches were also played with the Widnes C.C. who proved a little to 9 strong for the Warringtonians in that branch. During the season several week-end tours were carried out, all the favourite resorts in the Elles- mere, Farndon, Llangollen, and Sandbach districts being visited at various times. In the summer a great effort was made to secure a substantial sum for the Infirmary Extension fund by means of the fête at Hill Cliffe, and the Club undertook the management of the fairground, vialich resulted in a net profit of £ll6 4s. 9d. In the winter season several smoking concerts were held, and also two very enjoyable whist drives. Sixty-four were present at the whist drive on November 7th, and the prize-winners were :—Ladies : 1, Mrs. Watkins ; 2, Miss G. Watkins. Lowest score: Mrs. Delmage. Gentlemen : 1. A. Spinks ; 2, T. R. Critchley ; booby, A. 0. Clay. The other whist drive took place on December 18th, when 15 tables were occupied. The winners were: Ladies: 1, Mrs. T. P. Throssell ;2. Mrs. Bowoock; 3, Mrs. lay. Gentlemen : 1, B. Podmore ; 2, J. T. Sampson; booby, FL Denham. The annual billiard handicap was now approaching com- pletion. The 15th annual dance was held at the Lion Hotel on February 27th,when 110 were present. Possibly the principal event of the season was the dinner held at headquarters on November 7th to celebrate the club' 21st anniversary. The evening stood out as the greatest social success in the history of the club. The musical talent of their members and friends had in the many events of the season been again well to the fore, and the quality of their concerts, of which they were justly proud, had been maintained. The Hon. Treasurer's balance sheet would show the club to be in sound financial position which justified their policy of seeing that the members,by the adequate provision of prizes, entertainments. &amp;c., were offered attractions, and at the same time maintaining a sufficient reserve. The club had to lament the death of Dr. Braide, one of their members. Though the genial doctor did not come into close contact with the doings of the club, it WAS always a pleasure to them to have his highly respected name associated with it. Review- ing the events of the season, he (Mr. Smith) thought it might truly be claimed that in spite of unsatisfactory weather, the vigour and success of the club had in no Way depreciated. He personally thanked the committee for their assistance, and particularly the captain and the hon. treasurer for their valuable help. H e had to to express his regret that he found himself unable, through laisiness pressure, to continue in the office of hon. secretary. Mr. E. PICKIVANCE, in proposing the adoption of the report, said Mr. Smith had worthily filled the posi- tion of secretary and produced a report worthy of the traditions of the club. He regretted Mr. Smith felt it necessary to relinquish his office. Mr. C. H. WOOD seconded, and said the club had been fortunate in having been conducted by first-class men. Their success was largely due to the skilful way in which the affairs had been managed. The report was adopted. THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. GARNETT, hon. treasurer, submitted the balance sheet which showed that the income was £4l 6s. 7d. The balanoo. in hand was £29 9s. Gd. as against £36 Bs. 4d. at this time last year. On the proposition Of MT. W. C. LITTON (One of the auditors) who said the accounts were a credit tn Mr. Garnett, seconded by Mr. A. Boon', the balance sheet was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>VOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. BOWMAN moved a vote of thanks to the captain and sub-captain. They could not have had a better qualified captain than Mr. Fieldhouse who bad not only led them in their runs but had also conducted their musical functions with great success. Sergeant Major Delmage had been a most able lieutenant. Mr. SAMPSON seconded and the vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in moving a vote of thanks to r. 0 IT. Smith said he had made a splendid secretary, and his services in cOnneotion with the fete at Hill Cliffe were invaluable. Mr. LITTON seconded and the vote was passel-,</p>
        <p>Mr. PICKAVANCE moved a similar comPliment to the treasurer and said Mr. Garnett had apart from his ordinary duties done individual work of a secretarial character in connection with club functions.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. BELLIAN seconded and this was also agreed to. Votes of thanks were also accorded to the auditors and committee, to Mr. Unsworth for the use of the bowling green at Sankey, and to Mr. Borleigh for placing the room at the Patten Arms Hotel at the club's disposal. ELECTION OF OFFICE IS.</p>
        <p>Thf) following ()Facers were appointed :—Pre.ident, Mr. John Naylor (re-elected) ; hon. vice-presidents, the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Messrs. J. C. Parr, Roger C. Parr, W. Pierpoint. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., R. Pierpoint, T. Mather (re-elected) and W. P. Rylands ; active vice-presidents, Messrs. W. H. Benito, W. Brown, A. Booth, J. Critchley, J. L. Davenport, W. Litton, W. Mountield, J. Podmore, E. Pickavance and C. H. Wood (re-elected) ; captain, Sergeant-Major Delrnage ; sob-captain, Mr. T. P. Throssell ; hon. secre- tary, Mr. J. T. •ftrapion ; hon. treasurer, Mr. F'. Garnett (re-elected) ; hon. auditors, Messrs. W. C. Litton and B. Podmore (re-elected) ; committee, Messrs. J. H. Smith, F. Fieldhouse, G. Bowman, J. Poole, W. H. Bellian, Hong'aton and W. Taylor NC.FJ. delegates, Messrs. E. Pickavance, A. Booth and J. T. Sampson. A RECORD NIENHIERSHIP.</p>
        <p>A vote of thsnks having been passed to the Chairman for presiding, Mr. FIELIMIOUSE obieryed that they now had a record ninnber of member 3. They had had fresh applications for membership, and this was a good augury for the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>POLICEAE.N ASSAULTED.</head>
        <p>POLICEAE.N ASSAULTED.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Monday, Harold Brice, of Farnworth- street, was sent to gaol for two months for assaulting Police-consta4le Huntington and Police•constable Sutherland on Sunday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ILL THROUGH NURSING MOTHER. Debility, Constipation, and Plies Cured. Amazing Success of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</head>
        <p>ILL THROUGH NURSING MOTHER. Debility, Constipation, and Plies Cured. Amazing Success of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>The strain of narsing a sick relative and the anxiety of rearing young children will frequently produce in the wife at d mother that terrible run- down condition which opens the door to so many worse complaints. Mrs. Lily Denton, of 50, Fir- street, Walklev, near Sheffield, was rescued from such an ordeal by Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. - " Through nursing my aged mother for eight weeks," says Mrs. Denton, I fell ill myself. I had also neglected my meals, and became very much constipated and run-down. Blind piles developed and gave rne great pain. I had a splitting headache, and went weak as a child. I tried many remedies, but my illness continued. "I was very ill indeed, and in great pain, when a neighbour (Mrs. Amy Swift, of 133, Industry Street, Walkley), strongly advised me to try Chas. Forde's Bile Beans. Mrs. Swift told me that she had suffered in the same way for many , years, and had anremia in addition from the time she was a girl at school, and Chas. Forde's Bile Beans had completely cured her. I went in for a course of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans, and found them act quite differently from all the ordinary Pills I had tried. 1 had tried others, but found that I had to keep on increasing the dose,and was getting worse instead of better. I found that Chas. Forde's Bile Beans not only needel no such increas- ing, but kept me quite free and regular4ured the piles once and for always, and in addiTion made me better in every other way. They increased my appetite and made me feel strong and well. Indeed, I have never had better health since a few weeks after commencing to take them. "Chas. Forde's Bile Beans in smaller doses- have made our little girl well in a wonderful way. Priscilla was then three years old, a thin, weakly, emaciated little one. Her limbs were reduced to skin and bone through a period of stop- page of the bowels and consequent convulsions. Charles Forde's Bile Beans built up the child to a fine, stout, healthy girl, and so toned up her whole system that the ceavalsions and fits from which-she suffered severely, and from which our previous child died, ceased altogether." To quote Mrs. Denton's own words, " Chas. Forde's Bile Beans gave me back my health and strength, and saved the life of our darling, her father and I are firmly convinced of that." The happiness Charles Forde's Bile Beans brought into the home of the Dentoas, they can bring into yours. G-et a box at once. All chemists, Is. 11-d. and 2s. 91, but see that they are the genuine and bear " Charles Forde's" on the label.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>RUBY FITIVILL. THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>RUBY FITIVILL. THE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. • Wakefield T. Hunslet* Bradford Norton* 2 3 13 Merthyr Tydvil 0 0 0 Oldham Halifax 2 5 19 Bramley* ...... ... 0 3 9 Huddersfield* Leigh 0 3 9 York* Warrington* Hull K. Rovers* 4 6 26 Dewsbury 3 212 Widnes* Wigan* 1 0 2 Salford 0 0 0 Broughton 1t.*... 2 416 Runcorn ... Barrow*</p>
        <p>A Welsh Team at Warrington. Saturday was the occasion of the first visit of a Welsh club to Warrington, the Ebbw Vale men appearing to fulfil a fixture in the Northern League. Both sides were well represented and as Warrington played practically their full cup team there was a good attend- auce of quite 4,000 people. b'ine sunny weather pre- vailed and the ground being in grand order some fast football was witnessed. Teams : Warrington—Tilley, back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Brooks, three-quarter backs ,• Jenkins and Lloyd, half backs ; Shugars' Thomas, Belton, Jenkins, P. Boardman and Heath, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>– WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. _ _</head>
        <p>- WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. _ _</p>
        <p>Ebbw Vale—" Olio" Jenkins, back ; T. Davies, Burgham, Kerton and Morgan, three-quarter backs ; Beynon and Di Davies, half-backs; Rodvvay, Sanders, Brain, Brown, Edmonds and Lyon, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. H. May, St. Helens. Warrington started against the breeze and early proved that the visitors were by no means their equals. The scoring soon became fast and furious and the, play was so one-sided that anything like a description of the movements would be a waste of time. Warrington were early aggressive, and brilliant play by the backs led te to Brooks scoring. Maintaining the aggressive, Lloyd scored for Fish to goal,whilst a minute later Fish again scorad. The Welshmen rallied, and Beynon dropped a goal, but Taylor responded with a fine try. Edmonds scored for the visitors from a forward rush, and Sanders next placed a penalty goal. Brooks responded for Warrington. who led at half-time by 2 goals a tries (19 paints), to Ebbw Vale 2 goals 1 try (7 points). Turning round brilliant form was exhibited by the home bacirs,and after Dickenson had opened the scoring Brooks end Lloyd scored in quick succession, Fish placing two goals. Taylor crossed, and Fish again goaled, whilst Floyd scored after brilliant play by Brooks. Warrinn6 ton maintained their advantage to the close, though Sanders placed a goal for Ebbw Vale. Result :—Warrington 5 goals 10 tries (40 points), Ebbw Vale 3 goals 1 try (9 points).</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
