      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S BULB SHOW.</p>
        <p>One of the most encouraging sides of the Beantiful IVarrington Society's work is the School Children's ranch, which is making excellent progress, and has already done much to interest the rising generation in the culture of the beautiful. Saturday witnessed the holding of the third annual bulb show in conmection ;vith the branch in the Cairo-street schools, and the large number of visitors were loud in their praises of the exhibits, which numbered 350, and consisted chiefly of tulips, hyacinths, and daffodils. A new feature this Sear was a section devoted to paintings and drawings spring flowers from nature. Eighty-nine children aad tried their skill in this respect, and their work was certainly very creditable. A collection of choice plants 141(1 flowers from the Corporation gardens added con- alderably to the brightness of the room. The show was 213eue4 by Mrs. Robert Garnett, of Penketh, Canon cotevenson nresiding. Amongst °there were :.—The I\laYor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst), 1/15v. C. Harvey Cook, Mrs. P. Garnett, Alderman ,ennett. Messrs. E. Wallington, NV. Mason, NV. H. J. "eukins, J. Longland, G. H. Smith (secretary of the I?'esutiful Warrington Society), and F. H. Longshaw Besretary of the school children's branch.) .„,burita the afternoon selections were given by Miss ;aornton's orchestra and were highly appreciated were the songs by Masters F. Sirncock, R. Lawton, • Furness and P. Fair. Mr. NV. 11. Payton was the aeeorOpan:St.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>- - -- The following were the prize winners : ,„ p 'alias..—First prizes : F. Gee and E. Hunstone. -eoond prizes : L. Crookes and H. Sergeant. Third Prizes : E. Hands and K. Sergeant. Highly corn- ))) ended : W. Gray and E. Earle. Commended : M. I.4ittlewood and G. Ingram. „Daffodils (Single).—First prizes : A. Pontefract, E. Veesom, M. Barber, D. Green, M. Tickle and F. Peel. econd prizes : F. Sheargold. E. Hnntstone and W. Varrison. Third prizes : C. Shadwell, W. Marsh and Sankey. H.c. : J. Farrington, J. Cook and J. onlbrook. C. E. Heesom, R. Taylor and E. Goulden. Daffodils (Douhle).—First prizes : E. Hands, E. ✓ate, W. Hird, E. Bowe, E. Hundstone, M. Littlewood, Sykes and A. Hamlett. Second prizes : D. Green, -"-• Ellis, G. Hatton. J. Furnical and J. Bradshaw. 'third prizes : M. Downing, H. Fraser. C. Glover and A. Cade. H. r : S. Cowan, L. Hazlehurst. A. Starkey and E. Lee. C : D. Sutton, E. Smith, N. Heywood and A. Pevitt. Hyacinths (Blue).—First nrizes : E. Birrell and H. Lsedforth. Second prizes : S. Barratt and T. Parkinson. pird prize: A. Smith. H. c. : E. Whitfield. C. : • Strange and A. Stevens. R.llVacinths (Red).—First prizes : S. Williams and A. /ZIPITin. Second prizes : A. Anderton and E. Bate. -6:l!rd Prize : E. Sudworth. H. C.: M. Heywood. „.• N. Pemberton. ilVacinth (Lilac).—First prizes : S. Davies and C. , 14.4;rV. Second prizes : R. Webster and H. Burrows. ' Orrdlnprizes: A. Hibbert and S. Cowap. H. c. S. 1-I,vacTnths (Whitel.—First prizes • F. Drinkwater 'L. Brownbill. Second prizes : •F. Wickham and Anderton. Third prize: E. Gerard. H. c. : M. C: A. Smith. .u▪ vacinth (Pink).--First prizes: C. Shepley and J. d. Second prizes : D. Ingram and E. Stanton. Tri aird Prizes : W. Healey, E. Wood, and E. Brimelow. "•„c. : A. Daintith. C. : S. Gregory, L °nen Class (bulbs not bought from the Society).-71, 3.o.tvainiteihnt ; 2, B. Parsons ; 3, A. Wright ; 4, C. Fair ,t.n ; Hollingworth. WI of Spring Flowers (from Nature).-1, D. Farrar; 3. G