      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MR. G. H. ROBERTS, MP. 1 AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. G. H. ROBERTS, MP. 1 AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>"Socialism In and Out of Parliament."</p>
        <p>Mr. G. H. Roberts, M.P. for Norwich, was the principal speaker at a largely-attended meeting held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday evening, under the auspices of the local branch of the Independent Labour Party, his subject being "Socialism lin and out of Parliament." He said it was particularly appropriate that they should consider Socialism for they could not open a newspaper or magazine of any kind without finding some reference to it. They in the Socialist mov?ment had contemplated that for many years past. They had known that as soon as their move- ment assumed reasonable proportions terror would strike the ranks of those who were concerned to uphold vested interests and class privileges,and that,i rrespective lof speculative politics, they would be found united against the onward march of the Socialist movement. They already had signs that the political conditions in our country were rapidly undergoing changes. He had long claimed that the real divisions of thought and inter- est were Socialism on the one hand and anti-Socialism on the other. He was strongly of opinion that we were rapidly drawing towards the time when those divisions would manifest themselves in the House of Commons, and Liberalism and Toryism as we understood them to-day, would cease to exist and we would have Socialism and Labour on the one hand and all who cared to array themselves against them on the other. Last Friday week they laad unmistakable evidence of that tendency. The Labour party's</p>
        <p>UNEMPLOYED WORKMEN BILL was not opposed by one party alone. They had there a most significant feature of modern politics—a number of Radicals defecting from their own party ranks, which was a thing almost unknown in the history of party politics, and he believed that division now in course of progress would ultimately eventuate in a con- siderable number of Radicals now attached to the Liberal party associating themselves with the Socialists and leaving, of course, all those concerned to protect their wealth and also undoubtedly a number of people whom those wealthy men could place under their con- trol. The division in that debate was not Liberalism or Toryism bnt Labour interests on the one hand and all those who were concerned to keep Labour in subjection on the other. He claimed for Socialism that it was the greatest religious movement that the world had ever known. (Applause.) It was freely alleged that the Socialist would sweep religion from the land. The Socialists came into politics as A RELIGIOUS PERSON, .</p>
        <p>, I protesting that a mere intellectual assent to theological propositions, mere attendance at a place of worship, association with a religions organization did not neces- sarily make people religious. In the Socialist view religion WAS intended to be held• by the whole of the people. Every day with them was a holy day, and the Kingdom of Heaven was not alone for the hereafter ; it was to be lived upon this earth. The eternal life with them was the divine life. They believed that they could intelligently ordain economic conditions that they could make it possible for the divine life to be lived among the mass of the people. Those who dared to come out and say this was an irreligious movement did so without knowing what religion was, or if they did know it they were capable of gross distortion in the charges they so freely made. (Applause.) Socialism was the only progressive thought of the period. Could they point to him anything in the programme or policy of the old political parties ? Could they assure him that even tariff reform was likely to solve the unemployed question ? If Protection was the solution, bow was it that in</p>
        <p>PROTECTIONIST COUNTRIES they had the problem just as grave and acute as we had it here? (Applause). Unemployment was an inevitable corollary of a system of competition for private gain. The only solution was Socialism, but they knew that Socialism was not coming in a day or a year. It was evolutionary. They were not, however, going to wait until they had established their heaven. They were going to claim the right to work for every willing worker here and now. (Applause). What was the objection of the Government to the Unemployed Bill ? They said it was impracticable. Why ? Because they objected to the principle of the right to work. The capitalist and landlord, to whatever party he might be attached, WAS opposed to the solution of the unemployed problem. If there were no unemployed in the community the working clauses would be the most powerful section of society. Would men then be content to sell their labour for 12s. a week as WAS the case in some agricultural districts ? Socialists claimed that because of their humanity, because of the inexorable law of nature that all must eat, otherwise they must die, everybody had a right to work. (Applause.) It was constantly, urged against them that the unemployed problem was simply con- cerned with the unemployable. Be would not deny that there were UNEMPLOYABLES AT ROTH ENDS of society—(hear, hear)—and there was a large scum at the top which was greatly responsible for the poor dregs at the bottom. He believed there was no man or woman quite so degraded and dehumanised but that the divine spark was still flickering. Give the most debased persons a chance and they could be restored to decent citzenship. Referring to such diseases as consumption, Mr. Roberts said we were murdering on the avers ge 60,000 people every year because of unsanitary houses, fever- stricken alleys, miserably poor wages and wretched con- ditions under which people lived. Consumption could be extirpated when we had solved the poverty problem. There would not be consumption under Socialism, when people were able to live in healthy homes and have a sufficiency of life's necessities. The millenium would never arrive by reliance on other parties. It would only come by the people forming their own party and working together for the people's good. The Socialist and the trade unionist had now united for the purpose of dealing with great social ills. Although the number of Socialist and Labour Members of Parliament was only 32, they had already left</p>
        <p>AN INDELIBLE IMPRESS on the laws of our nation, not so much alone by what they had done, but also by. what they had attempted w to do. The Socialists were going to continue their work for the unemployed and they would shortly make another demonstration on behalf of the age of the community. They would be told again that there was no money to finance a scheme of old-age pensions. They knew the incomes of the income tax-paying class increased on an average £25,000,000 a year. During the past ten years the incomes of that small class of people had increased by £250,000,000. The increase alone was quite sufficient to finance a scheme of old-age pensions. The nation was practically pledged to Socialism to-day. The trading and commercial classes acknowledged that their only salvation in 'regard to transit rates was nationalization of railways. The nation was already Socialist to a degreeand when once they had nationalised the railways there was no logical halting place between that and nationalization of land, mines and other means of wealth production. (Applause.) 'the meeting was presided over by Mr. Wardtnan who said Socialists were ready at all times to take any palliative measure that came their way hut their ultimate goal must be complete emancipation of the workers by the advent of Socialism. kApplause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Motor Lurry's Freak. z-Erm</head>
        <p>Motor Lurry's Freak. z-Erm</p>
        <p>Get this ink). your mind</p>
        <p>At 12.45 p.m. on Sunday, George Parsons, chauffeur, in the employ of the Leyland Motor Company, was driving a motor lurry near the Central Station when in passings tramcar his vehicle skidded. Before he could bring it to a standstill, it crashed into the premises of the " Liverpool Courier," knocking a number of bricks down and damaging the door.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Earl and Countess of Dudley.—Lord and Lady Dudley are amongst the most popular of hosts and hostesses, and the appointment of the Earl as Governor- General of the Commonwealth is sure to be a popular one in Australia. His love of sport and freedom from "side "are the two essentials which go most to ensure success in the Colonies, where also the practical com- monsense and energetic character of the Countess of Dudley will be equally appreciated. She is the daughter of the Qoaker banker, Mr. Charles Gurney, and married the Earl of Dudley in 1891. He is the second Earl having succeeded his father in 1885, and is only forty- one, so that he is one of the youngest of the Viceroys, he having been Lord Lieutenant of Ireland from 1902 to 1906. Lord Dudley is supposed to have an income of over £lOO,OOO chiefly derived from collieries and works in Staffordshire and Worcestershire, and besides owning some 30,000 acres of land in this country he has large estates in the West Indies. The Royal House of Italy.—The King of Italy has lately had his hands pretty full with the matrimonial affairs of his relations. His cousin the Duke of the Abruzzi, stands sixth in the line of succession to the Throne, and the Duke's projected marriage with Miss Elkins, daughter of the American Senator Elkins, raised all the questions that usually arise when a member of a Royal House proposes to marry someone not of Royal birth. The courtship—now said to be broken off—did not run smoothly from the first. Then the domestic affairs of the Duke of Aosta, an elder brother of the Duke of the Abruzzi, have been the subject of reports, which associate his name with a notorious Society scandal. The Duchess of Aosta, who was Princess ' Hdldne of France, and married the Duke in 1895, left Italy last autumn, and has since made some adventurous journeys in Africa, visiting Uganda and the Congo in company with the Hon. Susan Hicks-Beach. a daughter of Viscount St. Aldwyn. She has two sons, aged nine and eight, but it is said that she does not intend to return to Italy. The Count of Turin, also an elder brother of the Duke of the Abruzzi, is un-married, and there was never any foundation for the recent rumours of his engagement to Princess Patricia of Connaught. The first American Peeresses.—Many American heiresses have married into the titled families of Europe, but there has been only one instance of their marrying into a Royal House. That the case of Jerome Bonaparte, the youngest brother of Napoleon, who married Elizabeth Paterson, the daughter of a wealthy American of Baltimore. Singularly enough too, the , Marquis of Wellesley, the brother of Napoleon's great opponent, the Duke of Wellington, also married an American his second wife being the sister-in-law of the lady who married Jerome Bonaparte. She was one of three Miss Catons, who were the first American women to enter our peerage. One became Marchioness of of Stafford, the second Duchess of Leeds, and Elizabeth who became on her second marriage Marchioness Wellesley. Jerome Bonaparte's American marriage however, did not turn out happily, owing to the opposi- tion of the Emperor Napoleon who, after causing it to be annulled, compelled his brother to marry Catherine, daughter of the King of Wurtemburg. The Shakespeare Memorial.—Miss Marie Corelli, who it is said, knew Shakespeare almost by heart before she was twelve years old, has done not a little for the preservation of Shakespearian relics, but the little assistance she has received from the public in this cause, makes her doubtful of the success of the £200,000 memorial scheme. She points out that there have been similar proposals before, although not on such a grand scale, but nothing has come of them and she does not think from her own experience in the Own of Shakespeare's birth and death, that the funds will be forthcoming. Miss Coielli is a woman of strong likes and dislikes. She dislikes motor-cars, journalists, millionaires, vandalism, ritualism, and so on, but all these pet aversions are as nothing compared with her admiration for the genius of Shakespeare, which many public men seem to think should be regarded like that of Wren's. a sufficient monument in itself. . .</p>
        <p>A Toy Exhibition. —An interesting exhibition of toys of all sorts is to be held in the Day Training College of the London County Council next month. The improvised plaything of the slum child will be shown alongside the elaborate toys of the pampered rich, and there will also be a collection of the favourite toys of the children of all nations. There must always have been children's toys of some kind, and those discovered in Egyptian tombs, some five or six thousand years old, as the toys of yesterday compared with some that the world must have seen. But this is to be an exhibition of present day toys, and it is intended to have some educational value in showing the toys best suited to the child at sac)/ stage of its mental development. Toys which encourage the creature faculty, such as a box of bricks, are to be preferred to the motor-cars and expen- sive mechanical playthings which have to a great extent taken the place of the more simple and old-fashioned toys. Some children's playthings never get beyond a few sticks of firewood and an old tin can, but the happi- ness which toys bring is not to be measured by their cost, and it is more than likely that the Toy Exhibition will offer some useful hints to those who want to give pleasure to little folks. Khaki Again.—This particularly unbecoming hue is much to the fore in shop land, and we can only marvel that with such a vast array of really beautiful colourings women can be found who prefer to wear khaki. However perfect the complexion may be, khaki renders it muddy looking, and upon blondes it may be said to work its greatest havoc. The wise will ignore this freak on the part of Dame Fashion, and turn their attention to more becoming colours, among them being the new pale" putty " shades, that fail to suit only the very few. Tulle Bows.—With smart afternoon blouses many women are winding round the neck a length of tulle, which they tie at the back in a neat butterfly bow. A more economical plan to arrange the swathing, and how, upon a wired net foundation, fastening it with hooks and eyes beneath the bow. To the girl and woman possessed of a long neck or even one of medium ' length, this mode is most becoming, but seeing that tulle is a fabric so easily affected by our climate, there is no gainsaying the fact that it is not altogether an inexpensive one. An Old-World Notion.—A gold or silver ribbon bracelet, sewn with tiny crystal beads, or seed pearls, and finished with a little flat bow of the same, or with 1 an old-fashioned pearl clasp, is one of the latest additions to the afternoon and evening toilette. These bracelets are worn either high up on the arm or between the wrist and elbow, and have quite taken the piano of the black velvet bend beloved by our grand- mothers and great-grandmothers. Boots and Shoes.—Not very long ago, if one wanted coloured boots or shoes, one had to wait while they were made, but at the present time many of the best shops stock such colours as gray, putty, beige, dark purple, olive green, and navy blue. Unless one happens to be generously blessed with this world's goods, how- ever, it is unquestionably best to keep to black, as unless every detail is well and carefully carried out, coloured footgear is apt to spoil what might otherwise be a successful toilette. Then again coloured boots and shoes add to the apparent size of the foot, another reason for avoiding them, considering we are still advancing in this direction. A few years tack a 3 was the average size, a 5 being considered rather disgrace- ful. N owadays a 5 is the average size, and B's are frequently asked for.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>PILES AND FISTULA.</head>
        <p>PILES AND FISTULA.</p>
        <p>SA PE AND SPEEDY HOME CURE WITTIOUT OPERATION</p>
        <p>FREE TO SUFFERERS FOR 30 DAYS ONLY.</p>
        <p>Just Published. An Illustrated Treatise by W. H. V eno, F.S.So. (Loud.), explaining in detail the home treatment of all forms of Piles and kindred troubles showing how these dangerous afflictions may be easily and speedily cured, thus obviating any necessity fol ank operation. For thirty days only this instructive bood will be sent free to all sufferers. and applications shoul. be made at once to W. H. VENO, (Dept. P. 303), Cedar street, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SCAFFOLD FATALITY AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>SCAFFOLD FATALITY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Thomas Roberts, a young joiner, in the employ of Messrs. Moulton and Sons.copper smelters, and residing in the neighbourhood of Simms Cross, fell through the roof of a new dolphin plant and received such injuries that he died before his admission to the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ST, HELENS LADY'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS LADY'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Much sympathy was evoked at St. Helens on Monday by the death of Mrs. Coop, the wife of Mr. R. Coop, o, Church-street, which took place at Millersdale, Mill Brow, Sutton, yesterday morning. Mrs. Coop had been in a delicate state of health for over a year.</p>
      </div>
