      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 28, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 28, 1908.</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S REALM.</p>
        <p>DRESS-FAHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>SOME SPRING MODES.</p>
        <p>As soon as the weather changes we shall all be slipping on silk or cloth jackets, embroidered all round, with straight sleeves to the elbow, the fronts rounded off and very open. We shall also be favouring princesse gowns, or the semblance of them, which button down the entire length of the front, somewhat diagonallf on the bodice but straight on the skirt. When we resumed waists, we also accepted the gowns which dis- play the shoulders and the figure at their best, and we are abandoning the Japanese inspiration. Bodices distinct from skirts, for day wear, are a feature in fashion. but for evening many of the newest are put on all together and fastened at the back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>SPRING MILLINERY.</head>
        <p>SPRING MILLINERY.</p>
        <p>Two becoming new hats are shewn in our sketch. The first is a smart toque-like shape of rose du Barn i straw. The brim is bent in close to the dome-shaped crown at each side of the front and back, the small hollows thus formed being filled in with clusters of jet beads, con- nected by ropes of beads across the crown, and</p>
        <p>trimmed* at one side with two black ostrich feather tips. The second sketch shews a hat of fine black silky crinoline, the brim being edged with a narrow band of velvet. The high crown is draped with folds of black tulle, over which is arranged loops of large black beads, the two up- standing feathers placed at the side being of a 'deep Empire green, shading to a paler tone at the tips.</p>
        <p>„,....---,.. wr. As 7 _ lit 5 • )y0 v_ , ~., 0.1. Ni ~, • ~.....4600,/ oil' •- ” i „-qt,- ~.., /1/ 5,,,,,,'"" ~ -....,.., , \ • \</p>
        <p>k 4 • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SMART FOOTWEAR.</head>
        <p>SMART FOOTWEAR.</p>
        <p>Boots are coming into favour again very much, although it is unlikely that the convenience and lightness of shoes will ever allow them to be put out of the running. The modern boot is a dainty thing, much lighter than one could expect, and made of leather or suede as soft and pliable as silk. When the dress is in a darkish tone it is very smart to have the boots exactly matching. At one time this would have necessitated their being specially made, but now a good many boot-makers stock them in such colours as dark purple, olive green, deep red, and navy blue. Thfte last are particularly smart when worn with a .blue serge costume. One or two enter- prising people have adopted scarlet boots with black patent toe-caps. It deed hardly be said that these are people with very small and pretty feet, to which they wish to attract the world's at- tention. The Langtry shoe is having a great vogue in suede, especially in tones 'of grey and beige, to tone with costumes of these colours. But adventures, of this kind in footgear need to be carried through, remarks a Globe writer, with such freshness and smartness that they are never likely to become popular. Shabby scarlet boots, for instance, would be decidedly sordid, and the grey suede must be as fresh and sissooth as though it had never previously been worn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FANCY HATPIN.</head>
        <p>FANCY HATPIN.</p>
        <p>Fancy hatpins are the craze of the moment, writes our, Paris correspondent. Among the latest, there are none so distinctly chic as the French veil pins with Greek, prolls in imitation brilliants at the head, which are to be stuck in on either side of the coiffure at the back to hold the lower edges of the veil in place. Enormous heads decorate the new hatpins, a fascinating variety being those of hand-painted china, and of semi-precious stones, as well as of amber and tortoiseshell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>POPULAR SPRING MODE.</head>
        <p>A POPULAR SPRING MODE.</p>
        <p>The sleeveless coat is a prominent feature of the early spring modes, and very smart it looks when carried out in light pastel-coloured cloth, trimmed with pas.sementerie or soutache ern, broidery. The original of our sketch was car- ried out in a soft greenish - grey faced cloth. The short kimono sleeves were cut in one with the coat, and were bordered with soutache,</p>
        <p>(C ) A if4i X)1 '•.9:1 4 ' ' • . : . •, I 4, ' 10 ; 1 rv) 4 , . , 1 , ;1 , , g ~. 10 0 I' i'l,4/ i.,0 , /I 1 1 i, I t, i i 10 \ 0 : 1 ,‘ 01 1 i 01 40 i \ 1, ~ .</p>
        <p>• -</p>
        <p>embroidereci _ green, gold, and black, and finished with, green bull fringe. The revers are also faced with soutache and edged with ball fringe. The fronts of the coat are left open several 'inches, and are held in place by a small tab, which fastens with a large button just below the bend of the figure, the "skirt" of the coat is cut up in long narrow panels, and out- lined with strappings of taffetas of the same colour as the cloth.</p>
        <p>I 11 1 1</p>
        <p>1/</p>
        <p>p</p>
