      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LADY INSPECTOR OF PRISONS.</head>
        <p>A LADY INSPECTOR OF PRISONS.</p>
        <p>A new and important department of inspeo- torial work has been opened to women by the action of the Home Secretary in appointing Dr. Mary Gordon to be an inspector of prisons and an assistant inspector of State and certified inebriate reformatories. We cannot doubt, re- marks the Queen, that a medical woman will find much valuable woitk that she can do in looking nrter the management of prisons with respect to the health of the women and girls who are in- mates of those institutions., The experience which some educated women have recently ac- quired of the inner life of prisons tends to shew that in many little matters the health and corn- 'fort of female prisoners would be much better attended to if some qualified, kind, and sympa- thetic woman physician could occasionally visit the occupants of the cells. Before long it may be hoped that there' will not only be this one woman inspector of prisons, but that women will also be included in the medical staff of soma of the larger prisons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>POACHED EGGS IN BLACK BUTTER.—Break six eggs on to a plate, and dust them with salt and some freshly-ground black pepper; melt about four ounces of butter in a pan, and leave it °ti- the fire till a nice brown; then pour it on to and over the eggs, placing these in the pan, and let- ting them cook for two minutes; turn them as rou would a pancake, cook them for barely one minute more, ana pour on to a very not aisn. Meanwhile, reduce two teaspoonfuls of vinegar by rapid boiling to half, pour it on to the eggs, sprinkle them with a pinch of finely-minoad oaraley, and serve at once,</p>
        <p>• .• • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>BRICK-STREET MISSION.</head>
        <p>BRICK-STREET MISSION.</p>
        <p>Effort to Raise £2OO.</p>
        <p>With a view to raising £2OO a bazaar was opened in Brick-street Independent Methodist Church and will be continued until this (Saturday) evening. The money is required to clear off the debt on the church and judging by the enthusiasm which has been thrown into the work the hopes of the promoters will be realised. The sale on Thursday was opened by Mrs. Robert Garnett and the chair was taken by Mr. C. E. Parker. Last (Friday) evening the sale was opened without formality whilst this (Saturday) afternoon the opening ceremony will be performed by Mrs. James Dodds and Mr. W. H. Sherburn will preside.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ST;I:I!)Tr,efirOZDE RS.</head>
        <p>THE ST;I:I!)Tr,efirOZDE RS.</p>
        <p>The following are the stallholders : SINIOR LADIES' STALL :—Airs. W. Barton, Mrs. T. Gallop, Mrs. B. Warburton and Mrs. W. Wright. YOUNG LADIES' STILL :—Miss H. Hill, Miss C. Richardson, Miss B. Shaw, and Mrs. W. Syers. REFRESHMENT STALL :—Mrs. G. Appleton, Miss N. Corlett, Mrs. T. Ellison' Miss M. Fletcher, Mrs. W. Hibbert, Mrs. E. Roberts, Mrs. J. Shaw, and Miss M. Rowlinson._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>SENIOR GENTLEMEN'S STALL :—Mr. W. J. Hibbert, Mr. E. Roberts, Mr. J. Shaw and Mr. B. Warburton. YOUNG MEN'S STALL :—Hr. G. Appleton, Mr. A. Morris, Mr. J. Shaw, junr., and Mr. H. Syers. SCHOLARS' STALL :—Miss E. Barton, Miss H. Bibby, Miss E. A. Holt, Mrs. J. Thorniley, Miss H.Thorniley, Miss E. Shaw and Miss E. Wright. The Shooting Gallery was conducted by Mr. W. Yates, junr. ; the Hat Trimming Competition for gentlemen,by Mr. J. Thorniley ; the Nail Driving Com- petitions for ladies, by Mr. W. Barton, jun. ; the Bran Tub, by Misses H. Howell and E Wainwright ; the Galvanic Battery, by Mr. T. Bibby ; and the Room of Mystery, by Mr. G. Chamberlain and Mr. J. Dykes. During each evening an efficient orchestral band, under the able conductorship of Mr. H. A. Roberts, gave selections in a pleasing manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>OPENING CEREMONY.,</head>
        <p>THE OPENING CEREMONY.,</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in referring to his association with all the Free Churches of the town said he sometimes wondered himself with what church he really belonged to—(laughter)—but he was very pleased to be associated with them that afternoon. (Hear, hear.) He did not think that the world sufficiently realised what useful work the churches did in the community. They were the centres of power and usefulness. The life of that church and others was a source of power which went into the homes of the people and into the locality in which they lived. There were social questions, the question of drink, the question of the housing of the people and all other outside questions which affected very acutely the well being of the nation but the power of religion was a moral one which affected the minds of the people. It went into the heart and soul of the man. (Applause.) He hoped the stalls would be cleared and the amount needed, or more, realized and then the building would be clear from debt. (Applause.) Mrs. GARNETT said she herself looked forward to the time when there would beno what was called secularism and when the nation would be one great body of followers of Jesus Christ. Speaking of slum property, Mrs. Garnett asked the women to go round to the slum houses and give a word or two of advice to the mothers as to how to wash and feed their children. as many were quite ignorant of those things. She then declared the bazaar open to all purchasers. (Applause.) Mr. ROBERTS proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Parker and Mrs. Garnett for their presence. Mr. WARBURTON seconded and the resolution was carried unanimously. THE COMPEPITIONS.</p>
        <p>The competitions on Thursday resulted as follows : Nail Driving Competition : Mrs. T. Barlow. Oxo Competition : 1, Mr. J. T. Barton ; 2, Mr. G. Mayhall.</p>
        <p>, THE PROCEEDS. The proceeds on Thursday, amounted to .£4B 7s. ad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CHILD NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man Sent to Caol.</p>
        <p>George Hargreaves, lit, School Brow, was charged before Messrs. H. Roberts (in the chair) and J. Rymer Young at-the Borough Police Court on Tuesday with wilfully neglecting his four children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary suffering 'and injury to health. He was further charged with running away and leaving his wife and children chargeable to the Warrington *Union. He pleaded guilty to both offences. Mr. Browne who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., dealing with the first case, said the prisoner went up to the sub-manager at Whitecross and asked for his wages, as be was going to Musselborough in Scotland to work and asked if be would take him on again if he came back. The sub-manager said if he went, he went, and he would not have him back again. Notwithstandin,, he went away from his work and was paid £1 12s. lid., a portion of a week's wages. When prisoner was working be could earn £2 a week. It afterwards transpired that prisoner did not go to lilusselborough. On Saturday evening he came up to Superintendent Monaghan at Market- gate and gave himself up. He bad left no money for the children and when Inspector Cox visited the house the only furniture was an old iron bedstead with an apology for a straw mattress on top of it. The Inspector at once called in the relieving ()Ulcer, who ordered the children's removal to the Workhouse where they were at the present time. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. B. Shearer then dealt with the second charge,the facts of which were similar to those in the first charge. Inspector Cox said he had had the case under super- vision since May, 1907. He remembered prisoner going away in September. They were Warrington people. He would riot work.</p>
        <p>Prisoner r I acknowledge I left work when I ought not to have done. , Prisoner was sat to gaol for two months with hard labour—a month on each charge.</p>
        <p>Even young children as soon as they have passed the teething period can digest and enjoy milk and</p>
        <p>Brown er. Poison's " Patent " Corn Flour in thc form of milk puddings, baked cistard or milk soup. The admixture of this Corn Flour with milk renders the milk much easier of digestion, by hindering the formation of curd in the stomach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Gymnastic _Display.</head>
        <p>Gymnastic _Display.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the Borough Gymnasium on Monday evening, the occasion being the children's annual musical drill, gymnastic display, and distribution of prizes. The Mayor presided and the Mayoress was also present. Great praise is due to the director of exercises, Mr. A. Mottram for the way the members of the boys and girls class had been trained. Exercises with dumb bells bar bells, Indian clubs, were given in capital style, and the exercises on the row of rings, parallel bars, and vaulting horse were also well performed. Exhibition of rope climbing and marching were also given. Some clever jumping was witnessed, and Master C. Harvey Cook succeeded in clearing five feet five inches. The tug of war between eight big boys and eight big girls ' resulted in a victory for the girls, who succeeded in pulling their opponents over the line in the first two pulls. Ten little girls also gained a victory in a tug of war contest against ten little boys. An exhibition on the rings were given by Messrs. A. Mottram (director of exercises) C. Sinclair (champion), S. Price, A. , A. Fawcett, and F. Sykes.— During an interval the Mayoress presented the prizes as follows Class : 1, Miss M. Kinsley ; 2, Miss A. Delany ; 3, Miss N. Pemberton. Boys' class : 1, C. Harvey Cook; 2, W. Furnival ; 3, E. Parsons, Mena' class; 1, C. Sinclair, (champion) ; 2, S. Price ; 3, A. H. Fawcett; 4, H. Holloway ; 5, F. Sykes; and 6, S. Wt,ods.—Alderman Tinnion (chairman of the Gymnasium Ccmmittee), on behalf of the Gymnasium Committee proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress for their presence, and to the Mayoress especially for presenting the prizes. He said they were very much indebted to the Mayoress for coming there for the second year to present the prizes. Three hearty cheers when then given for the Mayor and Mayoress.—The Maycr replied on behalf of the Mayoress and himself, and said they had always tried to do their best for the children. (Applause.) - Miss A. Clayton presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
