      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER. AIARCH 28th, 1808,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. AIARCH 28th, 1808,</p>
        <p>Mr. GAUTREY has failed to THE PECKHAM hold the Peckham seat. Mr. ELECTION. Goocit, his Protectionist opponent, has been returned 'with a very large majority. But there are some defeats which are more honourable than victory, and this is one of them. If the sort of electioneer- ing which has gone on during the last ten days at Peckham were to become at all general in this country, then we should have to say good-bye to the fair and honourable tradi- tions which have up to the present usually characterised out electoral contests. Every- body who knew the local circumstances at Peckham knew as soon as the vacancy. arose that it would be a most difficult seat for the Liberal Party to hold. It is true that there was a 2,200 majority for the Liberal candidate at the last election, but that was due almost entirely to local feeling against the sitting member,Sir FREDERICK BANstrxr. Until the last election, Peckham had always been Conservative, but Sir FREDERICK BANDITRY managed to lose it for his party, largely because h 3 opposed the taking of the County I Council trams across Westminster and Black-1 friars Bridges. His interest was apparently I, greater for the shareholders of the 'bus com- panies than for the inhabitants of his South London constitutency, and the Peckham result</p>
        <p>in 1906 was due more to the determination to stop Sir FREDRRICR BANBURY'S misrepresentation</p>
        <p>Of the wishes of his constitu3nts than to any desire for a Liberal representative in Parliament. That was, of course, quite as well known to the Con- servative organisers as to the Liberals. It was also well known to the local representatives of the liquor traffic, and, as the Peckham vacancy was the first London seat to be fought eince the intro- duction of the Licensing Bill, they determined to</p>
        <p>leave no stone unturned to win Peckham, so as to Claim their victory as a blow to the Government) in the hope that they would, by this means, secure the withdrawal of the Bill. ItTheir methods have been of:such a nature as to 41attuat even their candidate, and one of the most reputable Opposition organs in the London Press has also thrown them over. Mr. Goocir felt corn Pe'led to ignore them at last, and the " Morning Post 'flatly told them they had overstepped the bclerlds of public decency. Old electioneerers who have been taking part in the contest estimate that enN'thing from £B,OOO to £lO,OOO has been spout by the Opposition to wrest this seat from the Government. The expenses of the caudidate are strictly limitsd by law, but there is nothing to pre. vent the Tariff Reform League, the Licensed Victuallers' Association,the local Publicans' League, the Church Schools Emergency League, the Coal Consumers' Defence League, and a host of other bodies, some of them made for the purpose, from Swooping down on. the constituency with a gang or hirelings, and spending any amount of money they Please without accounting for it. That ha been done in Peckham in greater measure than ,evet before, and under the circumstances Mr. '-'4ljraxv is to be congratulated on having done so The whole history of Peckham since it was a separate constituency, up to the heat election, is eenservative, and there is small wonder that, tinder the extraordinary pressure exercised by these outside associations, the seat should have reverted to its former allegiance. It will make no difference to the Government. lt is the business and the pride of the Liberal Party to tackle vested interests, which are inimical to the welfare of the people. The more the Licensing Bill is understood, the more popular wil it become. We cannot expect the trade to like it. The struggle which has begun is as to who shall be master, the public or the peblic-house. The PUblio-house has won the first skirmish by methods which will recoil. As to the final result, when the issue is fully appreciated, we have no shadow of doubt. As no less a person than Lord ROSEBERY has told us if the nation does not throttle the drink traffic the drink traffic will throttle the nation :rhe result at Peckham will have done good If it shows the forces of reason and temperance. that they must be prepared to fight for their cause hey have to face a relentless, powerful, wealthy "ganization, determined to prevent the State fl.lr resuming that with which it ought never to re parted. They must not shrink from the "gilt because they have a friendly Government in aver. Let them determine that the Government ik4tall be supported in just as full measure as they tha tqtacked. No reform are ever achieved unless bet 4,,a who believe in them are prepared to do be "e for them, and Mr. GAuTßßy's defeat should a clarion call to those who feel that no one 0:10) however powerful, should be allowed to Natpower the State. It is now or never that the te roust resume its own. Let this attempt . dr.the part of the Government to control the beelnk traffic be defeated, and the Trade at once 'fl9a complete master of the situation.</p>
        <p>In a few days we begin a McKumre's new educational year, and DtreerioNAL the Estimates for that 4"CONOMIBS. Period present one or two thoee significant contrasts with the 'or the financial year now closing. One of red„raost striking is that wherein the I:•44gest ju iiietion is shown upon the current expenditure. is d e aggregate, the grant for elementary schools £404,500. The reason is that, largely 'ine-Book statistics, the Board finds itself 1.611, in providing for 200,000 fewer children. "Ile attention has lately been occupied with •</p>
      </div>
