      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 28, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 28, 1908.</p>
        <p>the problem at what age children should begin l 1 RUNCORN AND WIDNES. school, and the effects of it all has been to put foru.oird the school age—in the public as well as in SCHOOL BOY'S PUNISHMENT'. the expert view—very roinch hearer five and six UNSUSTAINED CHARGE AGAINST A TEACHER. than it used to too. That is on all grounds matt e At Runcorn on Monday, Mr. Arthur Lindsay, head for hearty congratulation. Babies of betw ee master of the Parish Church Schools, was summoned three and five should tint, in any society, ha set t by William Frederick Hancock, of Speakman-street, lean) lessons. At the present moment a Conan • for assaulting his son, aged ten years.—Mr. Linaker tative Committee of the B )ard of Education ,s, tsetaacttclerthcaatnoeq 'tlh' uee sbdoa y, SaenbcroucaT,! 02nd, tiller. fiDnaye,rsassitantt inquiring into this subject, and its report, it i was a Proper, punishyment, but immediately afterwards confidently anticipated, will he ready befora Mr. Dty told Hancock to get on with his work, and he Easter. It is certain to he an imp ortnut docu- said he would not. As a result Hancock was reported merit. With " compr.)mi,e "in the air, there may to Mr. Lindsay, who, it was alleged, dragged him yet be some chance in the immediate future of out of his place and hit him with his hand across the shoulders. tie took him towards the door, concentrating on edoieational matters pare ant , still hitting him, and when begot there the boy pulled simple. Tole nation pays eneuzli for education ; a top whip from his pocket and said," 'There is this yet" through all onWic channels —the rat,4l is pit ;At :3") meaning, he supposed, that if he hit him again he would millicios to dernaud scrupuloas an I oersis-oeut car muse it. The defendant toak the whip from tho lad, hit that the best results are obtained tor the outlay hn the imo fire. uth A e left arm, and then told the boy to throw it or. Lindsay then went for the cane, and -- it him across the arm with it. It was also alleged that We have much respect for the defendant put the boy across the desk and thumped THE RECTOR ON the Rector of Warrington, i him with his fist. Later the saute day the boy was " LOVE." Le#A de n shoulder, Canon Willis, but NVEI must Cullen, who found extensive bruising on the r' with a lesser amount of bruising on the confess to being somewhat right shoulder, and bruising practically over the whole ' shocked by the nature of the sermon he preached of the space between the two shoulder-blades. The on Sunday evening on the text of" Love." Judg- ' matter afterwards came before the managers. but the ing from a contemporary's report the Rector first ! witnesses declined to attend.—Evidence having been I thiveeriia, defendant %Nw-alt:i calolerd,waiityll siziisd he .did_ not Littler of all emphasized the need for the exercise of love and restraint at the present time, and fist .—Mr. .. , (magios?'ratwelanlda one opf the school managers said there then proceeded to place an interpreta- ' was a mark on the right shoulder the size of sixpence.— tion on the Licensing and Education Bills ! Archdeacon Wood and Dr. Nl'Dougall said they did not which is likely to provoke anything but the tender consider the punishment excessivo.—The bench also passion. " The supposed motive of the Licensing , held this opinion, and dismissed the case. Bill," he said "was the Prevention of drunkenness, but he had come to the conclusion that it was an attack on property." Then he suggested, "fewer houses, shorter hours, limit of age for sale, and the absence of unnecessary attraction." We fear that the Rector's " careful consideration of the subject" is merely a figure of speech or a figment of the imagination, for if he reads the provisions of the Licensing Bill through he will find that it provides for all the reforms he enumerates and makes no attick on property that anyone has any right to but the public. He make., the same kind of charge against the Education Bill. "Its supposed object is the furtherance of , Education, but the Bill is really an attack upon 1 the Church." The Rector ought not to make ' sweeping statements of this kind from the pulpit , unless he is prepared to substantiate them with evidence. And, moreover, when he wishes to do a little political propaganda work be should seek a more fitting place and not introduce controversial topics under the guise of " Love."</p>
        <p>The loudest complainants PITY THE Poon against the Licensing Bill are MILLioNAIRn I the brewing millionaires, and to hear the wails from some of them one would imagine that their vast store of wealth was going to be forcibly taken from them and given to the poor and needy. Lord Burton nearly caused a national tear to be shed by his doleful prophecies at Burton on-Trent then Colonel Hall Walker, M.P., spoke despondingly of the " ruin, absolute ruin " which he apparently saw staring him in the face. Now we have Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., DL., emitting a groan and actually retrenching his expenditure. C.irres- Pondence has been published this week—doubtless with the idea of touching the sympathy of the workingman—between Sir Gilbert and the Chair- man of the Belvoir Hunt Committee. The Knight of Walton has for twelve years been Master of the Belvoir fox-hounds, and the hobby has doubtless been an expensive one. This month he has written to announce his resignation of the post at the end of another season, and in his letter to General Willson (the chairman) he says : "You -must 'realize that if this revolutionary measure becomes law how impracticable it will be for me to continue the heavy expense of the hounds after that time." To the average man it looks very ranch as if Sir Gilbert had tired of the Balvoir Hunt and was using the Licensing Bill as a convenient " get out." It is not law yet, but if we are to take his letter seriously he has lost all hope in the Trade's fight for monopoly and believes the Bill will pass. This is distinctly encouraging for the temperance reformers. What a farce it is, though, for men like Sir Gilbert to write and talk in this strain. The next thing, we suppose, we shall hear that Walton Hall is to be let for small holdings, , that those fine fat pigs which are housed better than thousands of women and children, are to be sold for pork, that the valuable hackneys are to come down to the cab-rank, and that Sir Gilbert is looking for work that he may earn his daily bread and supper beer ! We could pity the millionaire were he going to be thus impoverished by the Licensing Bill or any other Bill. But the millionaire brewer will remain a millionaire even if the Licensing Bill becomes law in its present form—as we hope it will. He will still find a way of earning fat dividends and accumulating vast reserves of capital out of the sale of liquor. If he fears the contrary, now is the time to sell out and realize on brewery shares. Bass's touched a figure well above the average only a week ago, and we doubt not that Sir Gilbert Greenall could find:purchasers to-day for his shares ut a price that would relieve him of anxiety for the future and would provide even .for the luxury of leading the Belvoir Hunt —if he still wished to lead. Bat there will be no such sale, notwithstanding the " consfiscation and robbery " which is used so unblushingly as a text for deluding the public. The Beautiful Warrington BEAUTIFUL Society pursues its efforts WARRINGTON. heroically and, while War-</p>
        <p>rington still remains au unlovely place—as most manufacturing towns are —we can certainly take credit to ourselves for" some little improvement in the way of beautifica- tion. There is every prospect of the Society becoming a much greater power in the future, for it is going about its work in a sensible way, doing ' nothing ill-considered aud hasty which would I tend to needless irritation, but seeking to achieve its ideal by educational means. The wisdom of enlisting the support of the rising generation has been fully realized and one of the most pleasing features of the Society's work is that the School Children's Branch continues to make substantial progress. The third annual bulb show, which was held on Saturday, was on all hands•adrnitted to be great advance on previous undertakings and the happy children, who trooped into the room in such large numbers, manifested the liveliest interest in it. A new feature—and one worthy of every , encouragement was a section devoted to draw- ings and paintings of spring flowers from nature and the youthful artists displayed considerable promise. The Society had also been seeking to stimulate the enthusiasm of the adults by arrang- ing a series of lectures and on Mor.day evening they were honoured with a visit from Mrs. Higgs, of Oldham, who can claim to be the pioneer of the movement. She showed what a wide sphere of usefulness presents itself to the Society which is really determined to accomplish something, and it is a pity that she had not a larger audience to address.</p>
        <p>[Choir Sermons at Legh-street Church. 1 The choir sermons in connection with the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church were preached on Sunday Iby the Pastor (Rev. J. B. Buglass), when good con- gregations assembled particularly at the evening service, when a unanimous resolution was passed in support of the Licensing Bill. In the afternoon a musical service was held presided over by Mr. G. Davey-Smith who in the course of his address spoke in high terms of the usefulness of good choral singing at religious services. The programme was chiefly selections from" Elijah" and the following items were rendered :—" Hear ye Israel "by Miss A. Bridge who achieved great success with this difficult solo. Miss Mabel Henshall sang "0, rest in the Lord," in her own exquisite style. Mr. J. Hannan acquitted in "If with all your hearts "and "A dream of Heaven." Mr. H. Plumpton gave great pleasure in his fine interpretations of There is a green hill " and "Lord, God of Abraham." The choir who have a reputation for the excellent singing were quite up to their usual standard in the choruses, "He s watching over Israel," "Thanks be to God " and the trio for female voices " Lift thine eyes." Selections from " Hymn of praise "were advertised for the evening service, but owing to the indisposition of one of the principals, only one number was taken namely, duet and chorus," I waited for the Lord" tike duet being taken by Misses A. Bridge and A. Woodland whose voices blended splendidly and who gave a very fine rendition. Before the close of the evening service "The radiant morn" was rendered by the choir. Mr. A. W. Clatworthy presided at the organ with marked ability. Mr. A. Spruce conducted.</p>
