        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BERT BOOTS IN LINCASHERE.—Advt. Gas v. Electricity. At a meeting of the Science, Students' Association (Technical Institute) held on S tturday a discussion took place on the question of gas v. electricity as applied to lighting Mr. A. J. Jolley presided. Mr. H. Sherburn dealt with the advantages gas possessed over electricity in quality of light, cheapness, and hygiene. Perhaps his chief argument against elec- tricity and electricians was the fact that they had not thought fit to accept ' the Wellbach challenge. He spoke of the variations in electric light, caused by irregular voltage. Mr. Purse,assistant electric engineer, said that although he did not dispute the figures given he did not think it fair to judge by figures. He compared the two illuminants to an old bone-shaker at .-Cl, and a new three speed-gear machine at £5. He asked , how, if electricity was no good and not superior to I gas, it was being used in coal mines, on board liners. and in the best shops? Gas had been on the market , for 100 years, and was fighting for its life against its I younger rival. In regard to the Wellbach challenge, he said it was only a good and cheap trade advertise- ment. Mr. Sherburn, sear., spoke in favour of gas lighting, and Mr. Mathias in favour of electricity. Several students also took part in the discussion, after which a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Jolley for officiating as chairman. LINSEED COMPOUND 'of 40 years' proven ecffica for Coughs, Colds, and Bronchial troubles.—Advt. Concert at Bewsey-road. A highly successful concert, in aid of the forthcoming sale of work, was held in the Lecture Room at the Bewsey-road Church on Mond ty evening. The Re. Sidney Pitt presided over a late attendance. A capital programme of musical and other items had been well arranged by Mr. W. Healey, the guild secretary.. It was opened with a song by Miss Poveye whose pleasing soprano voice was heard to advantag in " When the heart is young." "The sailor's chorus' sung by the Male Quartette Party, was heartily applauded. Miss Upson admirably rendered a violin selection, accompanied on the piano by her sister. Mr. Walker, of Manchester, delighted the audience with his song, " The sailor's grave," and Hiss Faun y Mason who was accompanied by her sister,sang sweetly" Sleep my beloved, sleep." An appreciated item was that pro- vided by the quartette party, namely, rollicking chorus " Tom, Tom, the piper's son," and being encored they gave "How can I bear to leave thee." Miss Florris , Bennett's song, "In the merry month of May," proved a great. treat, while the humorous reading by Mr. Frank Norman, " How Billy Armitage got a night's lodging," c mvulsed the audience with laughter. A conversational interlude divided the programme,which I was re-commenced with a violin soh by Hiss Upson, I after which Mr. l'ovey sang " Down in the &amp;APT)" with good effect. A mandoline solo by Mr. Wright was, next on the list and later in the evening he gave another. Mr. Walker's reputation was again sus- tained in "Songs of Araby,' for which he was encored. An amusing duet " The spider and the fly" by Miss 'Mann and Mr. J. Hannan was an acceptable item, ! while Mr. Norman put the audience in good humour by the recital of "How Betty and me went to Belle " Tit for tat," by Miss Povey, was so well received that an encore was demanded, and in response she sang "Scenes that are brightest."</p>
        <p>Blitish Excess £31,000,000</p>
        <p>But even that does no justice to our case. We have no prairies to yield cheap food, no mines of such immensity to yield cheap ores, no cells to spout cheap oil, no warmth of sun to give us cheap native cotton. Americvi exports consist chiefly of Lod and materials, our exports consist chiefly of manufactures. We get a truer comparison in the following figures :</p>
        <p>..,unrch Ministers' Fraternal Northampton, wrote Commending Mr. Letheren to the love and confidence of the church as an earnest and devoted minister of the Gospel and to the ministers of the town and district as a brother esteemed and beloved among them. The President of the Committee of the Association of Baptist t2tinrches ot Nottinghamshire wrote stating that Mr. Letheren by the service he had rendered to the Baptist Churches had gained a warm place in their hearts, and they regarded him as one well fitted to carry on the work of God wherever he might be called. The CHAIRMAN, in the name of the Free Churches of the town, extended a hearty welcome to the new pastor and his wife. He trusted that their stay here might be very greatly blessed, that they might have a fruitful and mend life. He was sure the people amongst whom they had come to labour would give them that support and co-operation without which the efforts of any pastor, however able and earnest, would be of little avail. He trusted that this coming amongst them of a new pastor might e the beginning of a larger life for their church and people. (Applause.) Mrs. IttoNs, who along with Mrs. Robins came as a deputation from the Eingthorpe Women's Circle, said she could not express to them that night the feelingsof her heart in being sent there to speak of one so earnest and so zealous as Mrs. Letheren. During the years she had known her she had never found so zealousa worker. She (Mrs. Irons) bad met a great many women but she had never found one to equal Mrs. Letheren. She appealed to the congregation to give Mrs. Letheren 00-operation and the women of the church must do all in their power to help her in her work. She was con- fident that Mrs. Letheren was capable of carrying out anything she put her mind to if they would help and give her enconr !gement. Mrs. Rouss endorsed all that Mrs. Irons had said. She said she had never found a woman with more spirit than Mrs. Let berets. She believed alr. and Mrs. Letheren were really good, honest people of God. The Rev. H. 3. PICKETT (Northampton) said he lived not far from e here Mr. and Mrs. Letheren lived and had always found them thorough hard-working church- people. It had been his privilege to know Mr. and Mrs. Letheren for a considerable period and they wore true Christians who devoted their time to Jesus Christ and the liingdotn of God. (Hear, bear.) They must be allowed to carry out their own programme for they were well advanced in Church ideals. They were evangelical. (Applause.) Mr. Letheren would preach the truth and he would be loyal to the Church to which he had the honour to belong. He hoped Mr. Letheren would stay a long time in Werrington and that his ministry would be as prosperous as it would he lengthy He trusted they would give the new pastor and his who their loving and cordial co-operation. (Applause). The Rev. H. V. THOMAS (secretary of the Lancashire and Cheshire Association of Baptist Churches) on behalf of the Association extended a cordial welcome to Mr. and Mrs. Letheren,vrhom,he said, he had had the pleasure of knowing for a number of years. The Rev. E. E. Wwrert of New Brighton, said Mr. and Mrs. Letheren would find a very warm welcome in Lancashire. The minister's work was to preach and that Mr.Letheren was quite capable of doing.tle must not be tied down but be must be allowed to use his own judgment in what be said, he must be allowed liberty and then they would end him a good leader. (Applause.) The Rev. A. E. ROBINSON (Manchester), endorsed all that the previous speakers had said and regretted the absence of Alderman Barlow who would have been able to testify as to what Mr. Letheren c)iild do. Mr. Letheren would get a warm welcome in Lancashire but •of course nothing could heat Northamptonshire. (Laughter.) Mr. J. flartnrsolv (Liverpool, in the absence of Dr. J. Louis Fenn, Liverpool), said he had been very much struck with the character which bad been given to .Mr. -</p>
