        <p>and Mrs. Letheren. Anybody who came o Lancashire was always accorded a hearty welcome. He wished Mr. and Mrs. Letheren every success in their new sphere of office. (Applause.) The Rev. JOHN YONGE, on behalf of the Warrington Free Church Council, extended a cordial welcome to the new pastor and his wife. He said that they were receiving two true Christian ministers. He had been in Warrington 32 years and in Lancashire for 42 years. and he considered there was no place in England like Lancashire; and they would not find anyone to beat Lancashire folk. (Applause.) ,fo The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS brought forward a resolu- tion which had been passed at the last meeting of the local Baptist Churches welcoming Mr. and Mrs. Letheren. Mrs.LETfakrinx, who was cordially received,expressed sincere thanks on behalf of Mr.Letherenand herself for the hearty welcome which had been extended to them. She promised that they would do all in their power to further the interests of the Church, and said it was her intention to begin a Women's Circle in War- rington as soon as they could obtain a suitable room. The Rev. R. E. LETHEREN also briefly returned thanks, and said they would do their utmost to please the people, to please the Church and to please God„ He thanked them sincerely for the warmth with which Mrs. Letheren and himself had been received. On the proposition of Mr. E. ‘VYNICK-JONES, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman, speakers, and all who had assisted to make the proceedings so successful.</p>
