      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FAST ALLAN LINERS-TURBINES QUICK PASSAGE.</head>
        <p>FAST ALLAN LINERS-TURBINES QUICK PASSAGE.</p>
        <p>The. Allan Line turbine steamer Virginian, which arrived in the Mersey on Saturday, made a smart run across the Atlantic. Her passage occupied just under five and a half days. She left Halifax about one a.m.. on Sunday last week, and was resorted at noon on Friday to be 110 miles west of the Fastnet. This is the first round trip of the season made by the Virginian, I which is one of the fastest and most comfortable Istreamers on tile Canadian route. The Victorian, from Liverpool, was reported abeam of the Fastnet at 2.25 on the llth inst., and reached Halifax early on Friday morning, so that both the turbines have made, on the season's initial trips, splendid performances. 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ESPORTS OF M.INUFACTURES ONLY, 1007.</head>
        <p>ESPORTS OF M.INUFACTURES ONLY, 1007.</p>
        <p>British Exports (40,0)0,000 people) 342,000,000 American Exports (89,000,000 people) 130,000,000</p>
        <p>British Excess 212,000,000</p>
        <p>Now it is a really extraordinary thing that in 1907 America should not yet have reached an exportation of manufactures one half as great as our own. Her exportation ought by now to be fortiaidable, but it is not. And there is only one explan tion of the fact ;it is that America herself restricts ker cpnomerce by her tariff: linable to produce cheaply, she forfeits the leading p )sition in the world of trade which might be hers.</p>
        <p>Supplier not Competitor.</p>
        <p>The above American figures relate to exports from the United States to all the world. When we examine her exp3rts ta the United Kingdom alone we begin to realise how much we are indebted to her. America is not so ranch a competitor as a supplier. lam not able to analyse the 1907 figures yet, because full details are not yet published, but we have those of 1900 and., they will serve just as well. In 1900 Britain imparted £131,000,000 worth of food, materials and manufactures from America, largely, be it observed, in our own ships, so that America had to pay us for carrying her own produce. Let us get an idea of what tha _4:131,090,000 was made up of. First as to food : INtronTs OF FOOD FROM AMERICA, 1906.</p>
        <p>Butter 748,000 Cheese Wheat BOlO,OOO Wheatmeal 4,743,000 Maize 4,604,000 Maizemeal 169,000 Oatmeal 311,000 Fish 628,000 Fruit Lard ........ ..... , Meat °Hoak° Poultry Sugar 907,000</p>
        <p>Total with other items £31,681,000</p>
        <p>Next as to raw materials of industry : IMPORTS OF RAW AIATERIAIS FROM AMERICA, 190 G.</p>
        <p>Cotton Copper Silver Zinc 310.000 LeAd Other Aletals Oils</p>
        <p>Paraffin Wax 1,018,000 Resin 618,000 Seeds Skins and Furs 1,123,000 Manures . Wood Tallow</p>
        <p>Total with ether materials ... £55,523,000</p>
        <p>So, between food and raw materials as much as £111,000,000 is accounted for. The balance £20,000,090 worth, is only as to £15,000,000 manu- factures, but here aglin, the greater part consisted of materials of industry, as for instance, leather, £3,800,000 ; paper, £178,000 ; machinery, .£3,200,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Snother Striking eontrast.</head>
        <p>Snother Striking eontrast.</p>
        <p>fa a whole year, it is seen, kineriea sends to this country only S-15,000,000 of manufactured articles at c.i.f. prices —prices, that is including shipping charges paid to ourselves. Oa the other hand, in 1906, our exports to America, almost entirely manufactures, amouote I to ...`?8 ,000,000 at prices exclusive of freight and insurance. Conclusion Extraordinary.</p>
        <p>So we reach this extraordinary conclusion, that we dump more man ufactures into the United States, in spite of the Dingley Tariff, than the United States can ,dump into our free ports. It is a conclusion to give pause to any Tariff Reformer, hut unfortunately it is not easy to reach Tariff Reformers. The Unionist is just now fed upon tales of unemployment caused by imports of manufactured goods, and it is difficult to make him understand the real facts of the case, which are that we are the greatest dumcers in the world. If it were true that impor.s of manufactures cause unemployment we should then be the greatest sinners in the world. Luckily, we have no need to ; reproach ourselves. While it is true that we export , almost as many manufactures as any other two nations. put together, we need not fear that we are injuring the nations which buy of us. Trade, so it be honest, harms no one, and when a man, Briton or foreigner, , has commerce with the great place called abroad, he may bs happy to think that he does no injury to the nation of which he is a unit. If Protection were true every shipload of exports which leaves a British port would spell destitution for British colonists or foreigners It is fortunate that Protection is not true.</p>
        <p>No Ocean Ships. And then, again, there is the important question of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Occasional Court, on Saturday, before Mr. E. T. Evans, William Ronayne, of Newton, was summoned for being drunk, and was fined including costs.—On Monday William Collins. of Earlestown, was fined 2s. 6d. and costs for a similar offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL BAZAAR AT HAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL BAZAAR AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>A three days' bazaar has been held in the Eleydock Wesleyan Schools for the purpose of raising £5OO towards the cost of new Wesleyan Sunday schools. The amount raised was £527.</p>
        <p>WELSH RE-UNION.</p>
        <p>A social evening was spent in the Town Hall on Thursday night last week, about 60 members of the different chapels attending. An excellent tea was pro- vided, and afterwards songs were rendered by Misses Grissold and Margery Williams, the recent winner in the Earlestown Eisteddfod, a recitation by Mr. Platt. The humorist of the evening was Mr. Stokes. After the musical part of the programme, games and competi- tions were indulged in, including a competition for sharpening pencils by 12 lady competitors, Miss Watkins winning, and Miss Evans being second. For trimming a lady's hat nine gentlemen entered, result being, Ist Mr. Rowson, 2nd gr. Spertrnan, 3rd Mr. Williams. The accompanist was Miss Profit, and Mr. A. Pierce acted as chairman. DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS. There was no contest in any of the Earleatown or Newton Wards last Thursday, the following being re-elected without opposition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>criciw. LANE WARD.</head>
