        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE</p>
        <p>2. Cycling Club Meeting. Railway Games Competition Short Story. Local Football. 3. Local Parliament. Children's Bulb Show. Mr. G. H. Roberts, M.P. at Warrington. Brick-street Bazaar. 6, In the Seventeen Hundreds by J. C. S. Infirmary Governors' Meeting. Padgate and Penketh News. Ambulance Competition. 7. Science Notes, &amp;c. ASHLRY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. "The Voice of Labour." - - • •</p>
        <p>Owing to the pressure on our space of Licensing and other matters we have been compelled to hold over the above column. We will try to find room for the contribution by " Seveer " next week. The Late Miss Willis. Jt is proposed to erect at the West End of the Parth Church an oak screen in memory of the late Miss Willis, and Mr. Segar Owen has gratuitously offered a beautiful design. LINSEED COMPOUND' for Cough and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists.—Advt. Church Society Addresses. Yesterday afternoon the Rev. W. Qaibell delivered ,an address to women, a large congregation attending. Hie subject was " The mother and her child." In the evening the rev, gentleman delivered the weekly address t,) men on " I don't care." Next Friday evening, the Rev. A. J.. Waldron, of Brixton, will I speak on " Christianity v. Secularism." For influenza Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/1i and 2/9.—Advt. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. Clergyman Leaves £24,009. The Rev. Henry Brancker, MA., of Wimbledon,who died at the age of 90, has left estate valued at .-E,21,570 Igross. The reverend gentleman was ordained deacon in 1810 and priest in 1811, and held his first curacy at I Warrington in 1840-1, being incumbent of Padgate from 1841 to 1846. ASHLEY'S' New Address 4, Buttermarket Street.—Advt. j LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Rays Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs Calds.--Avdt</p>
        <p>! Education Committee. The annual meeting of this Committee will be held at the Town Hall on Monday evening when the busi- ness will include the appointment of Chairman , and deputy-chairman, the appointment of a selected ; member of the committee in place of Mr. Henry j Roberts, J.P., whose term of office erpires on April 11th, and of the various sub-committees. ASHLEY'S, New Address 4, I3uttermarket Street.—Advt. For Chronic, Chest Complaints, Woods' Great , Peppermint Cure.—Advt. ! Newsagents, Booksellers and Stationers' Union. The members of the Warrington branch of the ; above held their second annual dinner at the British Workman Cocoa Rooms, Horsemarket-street, on ThUrsday evening. An ercellent dinner was provided j by the proprietors to which ample justice was done, ! after which the members spent a convivial evening. Songs, &amp;c., were rendered by Messrs. Brown, I Burgess, Critchley, Horobin, W. Litherland, Locker, Muskett and Weaver, being accompanied on the ; piano by Mr. J. Corst. The party broke up about ! midnight all having thoroughly enjoyed themselves. For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/li, 2/9.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Death of Miss Watson. We deeply regret to record the death of Miss Agnes Watson only daughter of Mr. John Watson, 3, Wilson-Patten-street, who passed away very suddenly . .</p>
      </div>
