      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>DOUBTS BANISHED.</head>
        <p>DOUBTS BANISHED.</p>
        <p>MINCHESTER MAN'S</p>
        <p>SPLENDID—YEsi"fiii.6-isii.;- TO TEE VALUE OF DR. MORSE'S INDIAN ROOT 'PILLS.</p>
        <p>It seldom happens that a more concise and clear-cut statement is published than that of Mr. George Paterson, Clerk of Works, 169, New Lane, Patricroft, near Manchester, who has written freely and without solicitation. His letter, which hears the impress of sincelity and truth, speaks for itself : You are strangers to me, and Ito you, still a deep sense and feeling of grati- tude is the only excuse I can offer for presuming to send you these few lines. For many years I have been a victim to a disordeled Liver, and its consequent afflictions; and nearly three years ago was induced to try Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills, a medicine I can now con- scientiously assert which in every way accom- plishes all that you claim for it. I must admit that at first I was somewhat sceptical, , being all my life abhorrent of medicines and drugs of any description, but now. after a three years' trial of Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills, I as frankly admit that they have worked wonders on me, and have practically speaking made quite a new man of me. Without doubt they are honestly what you represent them to be, and that is saying a very great deal in this advanced scientific age in which we live. So thoroughly satisfied am I of their astonishing success, I consider such a boom to suffering humanity should be made as well known as it is possible to do, therefore you have my full liberty to make whatever use you may think fit of this letter. I may state that 1 still continue taking two Pills every night before going to bed, and will do-so as long as I can get them." Once tried, Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills will be found invaluable. They get at the cause, giving a healthy action to the Liver and Kidneys, cleansing the stomach and toning, the entire system. They are a positive and perma- nent cure for Biliousness, Indigestion, Constipa- tion, Headaches, Sallow Complexion, Liver and Kidney troubles, Piles, Pimples, Boils, and Blotches, and for Female Ailments. For sale by all Chemists and Stores, price Is. 11d: per bottle, or 6 bottles for 6s. 6.d ; or will be sent by Mail, post paid by the Proprietors, the W. H. Comstock Co. Ltd., 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E.C. A free sample will be sent on receipt of a id. stamp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>1907.</head>
        <p>1907.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>LIQUID HYDROGEN.</p>
        <p>The honour of liquefying hydrogen with absolute certainti, and in sufficient quanti- ties for examination, undoubtedly belongs to Sir James Dewar. Lignid hydrrgen was first collc;l.ed in open vuesels by. this distill- guish4 chemist on May 10th, 1898. In the following year hydrogen was obtained in the solid state. Liquid hydrogen is the lightest known liquid, its density being only one-four- teenth of that of water. It is a colourless liquid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>SUPERHEATED STEAM.</head>
        <p>SUPERHEATED STEAM.</p>
        <p>A few years ago, says the Engineer, few would have admitted the possibility of per- manently and regularly producing steam at temperatures of from 550 deg. to 650 deg. Fahr. within the restricted area of the ordi- nary locomotive boiler. Now thousands of locomotives use this highly - superheated steam, and its use is increasing. By heating steam 180 deg. Fahr. above the saturation temperature, "lint steam" is produced. With this increase of temperature, the steam is dried, and the volume is increased. But the increase of volume is less important than the suppression of all condensation in the cylinders if the superheat is sufficiently high. Hot steam being a bad conductor, it also re- duces loss by cooling in the cylinders. It in- creases the hauling capacity from 25 to 80 per cent., according to type and structure of the engine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>AGE OF A FISH.</head>
        <p>THE AGE OF A FISH.</p>
        <p>It has been found by ichthyologists that the age of a fish may be read from its scales. These increase in size by annular growths, two rings being formed each year. The " Otoliths," or ear stones, which lie in two sacs on either side of the base of the cranial cavity, afford another means of determina- tion. Like the scales, the otoliths increase by two rings annually. Each spring, that is, from February to June, a white ring is formed, and each autpmn, that is, from July to October, a black one. Thus the number of either white or black rings in an otolith gives the age of the fish in years. In . the case of flat-fish, the latter method has been found more trustworthy, whereas in the case of the cod the scales give a better result. Although varying much in size and shape in different species, the otoliths show a remarkable con- stancy in the same species. Hence they are of considerable value in the diagnosis of a species.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>INDICATION OF LONGEVITY.</head>
        <p>INDICATION OF LONGEVITY.</p>
        <p>; In the medical world it is a generally ac- cepted fact that every person bears physical indications of his prospects of long life. A long-lived person may be distinguished from a short-lived person at sight. In many in- stances a physician may look at the hand of a patient and tell whether he will live or die. The primary conditions of longevity are that the heart, lungs, and digestive organs, as well as the brain, should be large. If these organs are large, the trunk will be long and the limbs comparatively short. The person will appear tell in sitting and short in stand- ing. The hand will have a long and some- what heavy palm and short fingers. The brain will be deeply seated, as shewn by the orifice of the ear being low. The blue hazel or brown hazel eye, as shewing an in- termission of temperament, is a favourable indication. The nostrils, if large, open, and free, indicate large lunge. • A pinched and half-closed nostril indicates small or weak , lungs. These are genertil points of distinc- tion, but, of course, subject to the usual in- dividual exceptions. CELESTIAL ORIGIN OF "ITHE DIAMOND.</p>
        <p>Sir William Crookes, F.R.S., has written ' a very interesting article in the North Ameri- can Review on the diamond. It has been said •!that the !diamond is a gift from heaven con- veyed to earth in meteoric showers. Sir Wil- liam Crookes thinks the notion not impos- sible, and he refers to the masses of metallic iron found in Arizona, which must have ,! formed part of a meteoric shower. In one of I these masses the late Dr. Foote found black I and transparent diamonds. This startling I discovery was afterwards verified by Pro- fessors Moissan and Freidel; and Moissan, working on a, piece of the Canon Diablo meteorite, has recently found smooth black diamonds and transparent diamonds, in the form of octahedra with rounded edges, to- gether with green hexagonal crystals of car- bon suicide. Under atmospheric influences I the iron would rapidly oxidise and rust away, I and the met/eoric diamonds would be un- affected and left on the surface of the soil, to be found haphazard when oxidation had removed the last proof of their celestial origin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>HARDNESS OF DIAMONDS.</head>
        <p>THE HARDNESS OF DIAMONDS.</p>
        <p>Sir Nr illiam Crookes has something to say as to the hardness of diamonds. They vary much in this respect; even different parts of the same crystal differ in their resistance to cutting and grinding. So hard is diamond in comparison to glass that a suitable splinter of diamond will plane curls off a glass plate as a carpenter's tool will plane shavings off a deal board. Another experiment that will illustrate its hardness is to place a diamond on the flattened end of a conical block pf steel, and upon it bring another similar cone of steel. If forced together with hydraulic power the stone can be forced into the steel blocks without injuring the diamond in the least. The pressure brought to Ibear in this experiment has been equal to 170 tons per square inch of diamond.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>AN UNFELT BURDEN.</head>
        <p>AN UNFELT BURDEN.</p>
        <p>Down in the coal black depths of the ocean, miles below the surface, there are swarms of strange fish and curious marine creatures, which live their entire lives without ever see- ing the light of day, for sunlight cannot pene- trate the ocean to a depth of more than 1,2001 t. These creatures *ever rise to the upper levels of the ocean, as some persons might suppose, because the attempt would cost them their lives. Numbers of specimens have been dredged up by various scientific expeditions, but not one of them was alive • when brought to the surface. On the con- trary, ' says IS'cienre Siftings, their condition was frightful, and shewed clearly the suffer- ing (if fish really do feel pain) that they must have endured on the way up. The bodies of the fish were distended almost to the burst- ing point, and the eyes were frequently blown from the sockets. The bones were soft and cartilaginous, and the meat was of a pulpy nature, almost ready to fall to pieces. All this .is due to the fact ~that these fish were made IO WITHSTAND ENORMOUS PRESSURES, whieh occur in the deep sea. At a depth of a mile a fish must bear a pressure equal to a column a/ water a mile high and as large in section as the surface of the fish. In other words, he must support the entire weight of the water above him. Such a column, if Only an inch square, weighs a ton; imagine, then, the weight that must be supp,orted by even a small fish. This pressure bears on all parts Of the fish alike, upward and downward, for- ward and backward and sidewise, and the only reason the fish is not completely crushed is because all the recesses and cavi- ties of the body are filled with fluids which are under a pressure equal to that of the water outside. When :n this great pressure, the bones and flesh of a fish become as hard and firm as thcse of any other fish. When the deep-sea creature is raised to the surface the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HIGH – PRESSURE FLUIDS</head>
        <p>HIGH - PRESSURE FLUIDS</p>
        <p>imprisoned .within it are not counter-balanced by an equal outer pressure, and so they burst their way out. The conditions of the deep-sea fish are not unlike those in which man has his being. We are living at the bottom of an ocean of air 200 miles deep. The total weight of this ocean on the earth is over 5,000,000,000,000,000 tons, and at sea level it exerts a pressure of nearly 151 b. on every square inch of surface. Every full-wrown man carries sixteen tens or tins weignt, yet we uo not feel this pressure, because it is exerted in all directions, upward and downward, ward and outward.</p>
        <p>Only</p>
        <p>L</p>
      </div>
