        <p>the next act, and quite as unlike the accepted ideas lof a dairy or a young ladies' school as it is possible to imagine. Tne piece has secured high commendation String its run at the Apollo Theatre, ; London ; indeed, it has been described as the success lof the London season . It owes its authorship to Messrs. A. M. Thompson and Robert Courtneid,ge, the lyrics have been contributed by Messrs. Paul A. Rubens and Arthur Wimperis, and the music has been composed by Messrs. Rubens and Frank E. Tours, names that provide a reliable guarantee of excellence. llt fully deserves all the good things that have been I said of it, for it is one of the best works of its kind ; staged of late years. The music is bright, the dialogue witty, and the person who cannot enjoy a ; hearty laugh during its progress is to be sincerely condoled with.</p>
        <p>The company that visits Warrington is headed by Miss Empsie Bowman in the name part "Peggy the Dairymaid" and Mr. Dan Agar as " Joe Mivens " (sailor). Ur. Agar has been repeatedly commended on his wonderful performance of this part and is known throughout the profession as the only rival to the great Mr. Dan Rolyat who originally created the character. In addition to these two excellent artistes, Mr. Richard Telford will play the part created by Mr. Walter Passmore, " Sam," supported by a company of over 40 people. VISIT OF NM AND MRS KENDAL . . . _</p>
        <p>I For three nights in the following week, commencing j on April 6th, Mr. and Mrs. Kendal and company will appear, this being their first visit to Warrington. They will present the following plays :—Monday, The elder Miss Blossom ";. Tuesday, " Still waters I run deep " ; Wednesday, " The Ironmaster." Seats i may now be booked.</p>
