      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. R. Benson's Company in Repertoire.</p>
        <p>rUE3LIC tisFEWEft</p>
        <p>TRADE CALUMNIES MET BY ARGUMENT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Progress of the Ffght.</head>
        <p>Progress of the Ffght.</p>
        <p>that the enormous capital of the brewing industry was to be suddenly wiped away because six persons per 1000 were addicted to drunkenness. He was not very</p>
        <p>convincing. Mr. Reid Walker, one of the three or more millionaires on the platform, struck a plaintive note. Of the 4055 persons employed by his firm, one-third would be thrown out of employment if the Licensing Bill became law. The Government had restricted their trade until little was left, and they now *wanted to take all they had. Mr. Reid Walker looked very miserable as he said this, but our bowels of compassion were not greatly stirred Personally we thought him "skinny" when he told the pathetic tale of me of his workmen coming to him with a record of 35 years service and asking for a pension. Mr. Walker kindly agreed that but itead of bread he</p>
        <p>should you ever do so, we ask you not to attach too much importance to the fact that a large part of our income arises from about 170 public-houses in Hertford- shire which we hold on annual licences, the renewal of the majority of which before 1904, was entirely and absolutely in the discretion of the justices; we prefer not to labour this point for fear of confusing you,all we care about at the moment is to make it quite clear to you dear children that we stop at once our help to your annual treats." _ _</p>
        <p>I trust it will not be thought that I in any way question Messrs. Benskin's absolute right to cut off any annual subscription without notice, but that is because I am not one of those who cannot distinguish between a hardy annual and a perennial.— Yours faithfully, A. HOLLAND-HIBBERT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Waif ord, 23rd March. Vested Interests v. The National Good,</head>
        <p>Waif ord, 23rd March. Vested Interests v. The National Good,</p>
        <p>THE BEER MEETING.</p>
        <p>HOW THE TRADE WILL BE RUINED. • A Criticism of the Speeches. The " beer meeting" in Parr Hall on Thursday evening was a great success. Crowds assembled inside to refresh themselves by the flow of oratory, and there were crowds outside to see the nobility and clergy file in. It has been a question of late where were the local clergy on this great question of licensing reform. The answer was found on Thursday evening. Many of them were on the platform cheek b, -4 with the people who annually send them</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 26, 1908. THE GOVERNMENT FIRM. PREPARED TO STAND OR FALL BY THE BILL.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 26, 1908. THE GOVERNMENT FIRM. PREPARED TO STAND OR FALL BY THE BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd-George delivered an eloauent speech in support of the Licensing Bill at a Ignited Kingdom Alliance demonstration in London on Thursday. With regard to the intentions of the Government he said : I have one thing to say, and it is really what I have come to say. If we mean to stop this evil we must put forth every exertion of which we are capable. Ministers will do it, the Government have started, and they are not going to turn back. (Prolonged cheering). If we knew that a fortnight hence, when the division on the Licensing Bill comes, we should be driven out of power, we would rather fall—(great cheering, the whole audience again rising and demonstrating their enthusiasm), and from that fall stand up and begin the fight again (loud cheers). We hava been beaten, and beaten badly ; but it is only the first skirmish. The war is but beginning, and we must see it through (cheers.) May I make one appeil to the Christian churches ? The responsibility is largely I theirs, they ought to be the foremost in the fight with- out distinction, and the more powesful and influential the church the greater ought to be its share in the fight 1 willingly, as a Nonconformist, recognise the</p>
        <p>NOBLE STAND MADE BY THE ARCHBISHOP of Canterbury (loud cheers) in the face of the sordid menaces of the brewers, who think that anybody can be bought with money ; who think that they have simply got to go to a clergyman,who has a responsibility which is the most sacred that you can conceive, and sty, " Here is a cheque, if you abandon the children to be trampled under the heel of drink ; here is a cheque for your church." Let their cheques go— (Loud cheer- ing, amidst which the rest of the sentence was lost.) Let us close our ranks—(cheers)--for the Master calleth. (Loud cheers.) Let the great Church, which is the official guardian of the moral interests of the people for the time being, take the lead. There is not a Nonconformist who will not follow, gladly in this great crusade. (Loud cheers).</p>
        <p>The tenant's capital was all gone and they ordered him to increase the consumption of drink and if he did not they turned him out. Evidence was given in one case of 22 changes having taken place in 20 years because the tenant could not get a living. Then when the Government wanted to close public-houses the brewers cried out about the injury it would do to these poor people. Then again the ratepayers were robbed by the introduction of bogus tenants who were charged about a tenth the value of the house for rent and the place was assessed accordingly. The trade told them that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MR. J. HOWES' ADDRESS TO JUNIOR LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>MR. J. HOWES' ADDRESS TO JUNIOR LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>An Appeal to the Progressive Forces. Mr. JOSEPH HOWES delivered an interesting address on the Licensing Bill at the meeting of the Junior Liberal Association on Nlonday evening. Mr. E. Wynne-Jones presided. Mr. HOWES fully explained the chief provisions of the Bill. At the outset he said he was very glad we now had a Government courageous enough to make some attempt to deal with the extraordinary state of things created by the liquor traffic. The first difficulty arose from the vexed question of vested intereit in the licence. For four hundred years it was admitted there was no such thing. Just before the late Govern- ment came into office they had the admission from the Secretary of the Licensed Victuallers' Association, after the Sharp v. Wakefield case, that there was no vested interest and that the licence was permission to sell drink for twelve months and no more. Those con- nected with the traffic, very generously for themselves, interpreted "no more " to mean " for ever." (Laughter.) Consequently they went for what they , were pleased to call a renewal of licences. By law there was</p>
        <p>NO SUCH THING AS A RENEWAL. Every time a publican went to the magistrate he went for a new licence. They repeated the word renewal until the public accepted it and then they talked about vested interest and the public accepted that. G-md reforms were frequently spoiled by reformers and the best of cases were given away by their own disciples. Fre- quently they heard good temperance reformers talking to the effect that "if there was no legal vested interest there was a moral vested interest." Fancy talking about anything moral connected with the liquor traffic ! Out of the question of vested interest arose the ques- tion of compensation. For many years the decent portion of the community—such, for instance, as the United Kingdom Alliance, which was not a total absti- nence society—kept the banner flying, insisting upon no vested interest and consequently nothing to compensate. Some men in the temperance camp, however, conceded the point and the Tories were eager to take hold of it, so Mr. Balfour's Act created a vested interest in the licences and provided that after the trade had plundered the nation all these hundreds of years and the nation said " Hands otf," the nation must compensate the robber because the power to plunder was taken away. At the time Mr. Balfour's Act was passed the leaders of the Liberal party declared their intention of undoing it as speedily as possible, so that be did not think their Tory friends had much ground for complaint. If they could pick a hole in the Liberals for not fulfilling their promises they did so, and here when they did fulfil their promises they also objected. Those were pretty Fanny's ways. (Laughter.) Mr. Asquith's Bill gave a</p>
        <p>TIME LIMIT - of fourten years. That was a terribly long time, for the Peel Commission appointed by the late Government recommended only seven years. The trade complained that fourteen yenrs was too short a time. They would make the same complaint if the Government gave them forty. (Hear, hear.) Members of Parliament who Were being urged to go in for a lone time limit should remember that the whole history of British legislation was that they never gained by making concessions to their opponents. Every concession brought increased demands. One good feature of the Bill was that the great reduction in the licences would commence at once, and go on during the period of fourteen years. It would have been very hard for smile who had spent a large part of their lives in advocating an alteration in the drinking customs of the country, if they had had to wait fourteen years before they saw an improvement. The arrangement, however, was made in the Bill whereby the licences should decrease annually so that at the end of fourteen years the number would be reduced to the limit mentioned in the Bill,ia proportion to the population. After the measure was pissed, any new licence asked for would be subject to</p>
        <p>LOCAL VETO and the local veto might be carried by a majority of one. That was in advance even of the propJsition of the Unite I Kingdom Alliance which used to suggest a two thirds majority—a great concession to the trade. He saw on the bills issued by the trade that individual liberty was above all considerations of morality. That meant liberty to drink and to have plenty of public houses at every corner. If they asked for liberty to close any of them, that was tyranny. (Lau.ghtel.). The supporters of the Bill would be accused of teetotali3m —that was the heinous offence at the present time. (Laughter). It showed how drink influenced the intellect and warped and twisted the idea of what wts right and wrong. The compensation under Mr. Balfour 's Act was provided out of a fund provided by the trade and the payment was on the terms or ten yaws' profits. Mr. Asquith's Bill prgvided that tho compensation must be paid on the asertained value of the licence extinguished and added that the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SUNDAY CLOSING</head>
        <p>SUNDAY CLOSING</p>
        <p>did not diminish drinking. If it did not, they 4vould have no complaint. They knew that Sunday closing did diminish the consumption, and the testimony was all to that effect. Continuing, Mr. Howes pointed out that the trade was very active in opposition to the Bill, and if the friends of law and order did not rally to the support of the Government, the forces of destruction end disaster would carry the day. He was glad to see that in this town an institution was formed, and seemed to be goinr, on very vigorously, and he hoped all present would make an effort to attend the ward meetings and demonstration which were being arranged. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MEMBER FOR THE BOROUGH</head>
        <p>MEMBER FOR THE BOROUGH</p>
        <p>was very strong on the Bill. Be had pledged himself to support the second reading, and was speaking very enthusiastieally about it. A deputation from the trade had already waited upon him. Special interests were always alive, but the great public weal was not always properly attended to. It behoved all right-thinking, decent, orderly and Christian people to rally to the support of the Member and the cause he represented in order that they might if possible get the Bill through, and have a cleaner existence in some towns in the future than they had had in the past. (Applause.) I The CHAIRMAN said that as far as the Free Churches were concerned he believed an effort would be made to , get a Licensing Bill Sunday in Warrington. Many churches, he believed, had passed resolutions already. He thought there was evidence at the present time that every public house was really a Tory committee room. I The friends of progress and temperance must be up and I doing in the matter. Liberalism had always stood for government of the people by the people and the Bill was true. Liberalism and progressive reform.</p>
        <p>;- Councillor HAWTHORN said they could not give too much assistance to the Member in the stand he was making for the Bill. It was a clarion call to everyone who had the welfare of England at heart to rally round the standard which had been raised and assist the Government. The cry of the brewers that the Bill would not lessen drinking was falsified by their own attitude.</p>
        <p>Mr.H. W. Guest,Ar. A. Tilling, Mr. R.L.Jones,Mr. S. IVright, Mr. J. Darbyshire and Air. R. Jonez also took part in the discussion an. a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to i‘lr. Howes for his address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MR. C. E. PARKER AND CONFISCATION.</head>
        <p>MR. C. E. PARKER AND CONFISCATION.</p>
        <p>Speaking at the recognition meeting held at. Golborne-street Baptist Church on Thursday evening, in connection with the settlement of the Rev. R. E. Letheren as minister, Mr. C. E. Parker, (President of the Warrington ',ilia Church Council) said 1)1r. Letheran came to the" good old town " at a very interesting time. They were engaged in a great moral struggle, and so far as the Free Churches were concerned, they were to a man on what he thought to be the right side. There was a meeting in the Parr Hall that night to which he had hoped to go—in fact he had promised to do so—had it not been for that recognition meeting. It was probable that at the Parr Hall meeting every speech would ring with the terms 'the rights of property " and "confiscation," It seemed to him that it was really questionable on which side the confiscation was. (Hear, hear.) There was an attempt on the part of "the trade" to monopolise a value which had always belonged to the State, which the State had never parted with and for which "the trade" had never paid an equivalent value. Therefore he thought they ought to look at it from that standpoint—the confiscation was really an attempt by the trade to confiscate property which rightly belonged to the State. (Hear, hear.) No doubt at the Parr Hall meeting they would hear of the unfortunate state of those pastors who bad invested their little all—it was generally little all—(laughter)— in brewery shares.lt seemed to him that any pastor who had invested his money on a fictitious value must have had a great deal more than he knew what to do with— (laughter)—but while there was the question of property rights which ought to be respected, there was the question of human rights which must stand above and in front of property rights. (Hear,hear.) They ought to consider not only the poor pastors who had invested their little all in brewing debentures but also the other pa.,tors whose work of love was handicapped at every tura by the drink traffic. (Hear. hear.) There was not a pastor who, if he was doing his proper work, was not , again and again disheartened and thrown back because he felt there was fighting against him the influence of the drink traffic. They would hear probably about the widows who had invested their little all, but they ought also to consider another class of widows who were so because of the result of intoxicants. (Applause.) The Rev. H. J. PICKETT of Northampton speaking on the same subject, said he was against confiscation and against the meeting in the Parr Hall which was being held that night. If he had not been at the recognition services, he would have been at a temperance meeting down in Northampton to support the Government Licensing Bill because he thought it was going to help the churches. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Resolutions in Support. At the meeting of the Primitive Methodist Council embracing Warrington, Runcorn, and Frodsharn, and representing a large number of churches, the following resolution was passed and directed to be sent to the Chancellor of the Exchequer, the Leader of the opposi- tion, and the members of Parliament whose con- stituPrwies are included in the Council's area</p>
        <p>" That this Council recognises with deep gratitude the courageous way in which the Government has approached the Licensing Question, desires to express its determination to give it every support, and would unze upon it the necessity of standing firm on the question of the Time Limit and control of clubs," INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, BRICK STREET</p>
        <p>_ At the close of the evening service on Sunday last the followinz resolution was submitted and carried unanimously_ .</p>
        <p>- . - That this meeting of the members and congregation of the above church, heartily welcomes the Licensing Bill as a great measure of Temperance Reform, and rejoices that it provides for the immediate application of the principle of local option to new Licences, and for the extension of the same principle automatically to all licenses after 14 years. This meeting further assures the Government of its strenous support in their efforts to pass the Bill into Law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>NEXT MONDAY'S WARD MEETING.</head>
        <p>NEXT MONDAY'S WARD MEETING.</p>
        <p>Those who want to hear the truth about the Licensing Bill phould make a point of attending the public meet- arranged by the Liberal Association which will be held in Thewlis-street schools on Monday evening. Mr. C. E. Parker will preside and addresses will be delivered by Mr. W. Madden, Mr. J. Upson, and Mr. J. Howes. FORTHCOMING PARR HALL DEMONSTRATION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist." TO-DAY'S CUP-TIE.</p>
        <p>Warrington's fate as possessors of the NAT. Cup is to-day trembling in the balance. They face a very severe task at Broughton, and candidly speaking the odds must be considered to be on the side of the Rangers. But lam far from being without hope of Warrington's success. They are fine cup-fighters, with a better record in the Competition than Broughton, and if they do at any time really give their best it is in a knock-out contest. Moreover, they won the Cup on the Broughton ground a year ago, and they drew with Bradford there a season or two before that. Better still they once met their present opponents on the same field and made a draw,which was converted into a win in the re-play at Warrington. The omens there- fore do not point to a Warrington defeat, and I shall go to Broughton this afternoon with considerable confidence in the ability of the Wirepullers to, at least, hold their own.</p>
        <p>It is very unfortunate that for such an encounter Tilley will be absent. The full back has covered himself with glory in cup-tie struggles and his dis- play in the final a year ago will not readily be forgotten. It was hoped that the injury sustained in the last few minutes of the game with Ebbw Vale would not prove serious, but a sprained ankle requires something more than seven days to recover its strength and the popular Widnesian has been compelled to stand down. Jordan has been given the vacancy, and this dashing young player may be trusted to fill the breach with credit.</p>
        <p>Tilley's injury is doubly unfortunate because Jordan would otherwise have had the right wing position. Seeing that he was not available,l think the Committee made a mistake in substituting Isherwood for Watkin. The former never could play with success anywhere but at centre,and I cannot imagine him being better on the defence or as good on the attack as the man he dis- places. In all probability it will result in Taylor and Isherwood having to change places at the interval with a consequent weakening of the whole three- quarter line—hitherto the strongest part of the team. I don't like this swapping of horses when crossing the stream.</p>
        <p>The selected team is as follows : Jordan, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor, and Isherwood, three- quarters ; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs ; Thomas, Shugars, Jenkins, Belton, P. Boardman, and Naylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>The men have pAid a visit to the brine 13%ths at Northwich this week, and every one is reported fit as a fiddle. If the forwards really do their full share of work in the pack, the team should hold the Rangers. The latter are very strong in the scrummage, and they will need to be mastered here for Warrington to do any good. The forwards must realise this and4act upon it if they wish to finger another cup medal.</p>
        <p>Broughton have provisionally chosen the following fourteen :—Barlow ' • llardyman, Wilson (captain), Flogg,C. James ; S. James, Flynn ; Ruddick,Beetham, Clampitt, Darlison, Winskill, Grainey and Scott. This is the best side available, with the exception of Mottram, injured.</p>
        <p>The Warrington " A "team to meet St. Helens " A " at Wilderspool in the Lancashire Shield Com- petition will be ; —Brarnhall ; Landor, Watkin, Cart- wright, Tinckler ; Daintith, Jenkins ; Harmer, Forster, Jolley, Kettle, Holbrook, and Fairhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WORKS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE WORKS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The first round of the Rugby Works Competition commenced on Monday evening and the following are the results up-to-date :—Monday, Armitage and Rigby 3, Richmond's (Academy-street), 3. Tuesday, L. and N.-W. Railway 13, Ryland's Apprentices 0. On Wednesday the match between Longford and Oros- fields' Soap works was abandoned owing to the rain. Thursday, P. and K. (Bewsey) 0, Walker's Brewery 0. Friday, Richmond's 29,1\lersey Lead Company 0.</p>
        <p>THE LEAGUE. For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'ege. Oldham Hunslet Broughton 26 19 6 1 51 83 373 27 37 165 7500 Wigan 2B 19 8 1 59 99 415 32 37 175 69'10 Sal ford Hull K. R. Halifax Warrington Batley 29 18 11 0 54 67 309 46 63 281 62•06 Wakefield T 2B 16 11 1 55 81 353 51 62 288 6034 Keighley 27 15 12 0 51 58 276 50 58 274 55.55 Bradford 29 16 13 0 46 62 278 41 77 313 5517 Barrow Hull 29 14 15 0 50 69 307 42 58 258 48.27 Runcorn 27 13 14 0 23 58 220 26 48 196 4'314 Dewsbury 2B 12 15 1 45 52 216 42 69 291 44.61 Rochdale Huddersfleld Leeds York Leigh Swinton [Merthyr St. Helens 27 6 19 2 31 39 179 48110 426 2592 Widnes Ebbw Vale 25 519 1 21 28 126 53 83 355 2200. Bromley...</p>
        <p>Warrington had a preliminary canter for to-day's big cup-tie at the expense of Ebbw Vale last Saturday. The latter were, I believe, the first Welsh club to visit Wilderspool and as pioneers they had to suffer considerable hardship. Warrington unceremoniously ran up the huge score of 40 points to %but the Welsh- men took the licking in very good part. They have not done badly in their first season under the Northern Union and few clubs have beaten them so decisively as the Cup-holders did. Moreover they are not without evidences of improvement</p>
        <p>Di Davies, who played for Warrington when they first won the Cup, was the mainstay of the Ebbw Vale back division, and on Saturday be ~played as well as ever he did in his life. Di has worn the colours of Salford, Warzin.gton, Huddersfield, and Ebbw Vale in succession, but be is far from the veteran stage even yet. Toni Davies, late of Leigh, also played a fair game on the left wing, while at full hack" Chick " Jenkins was quite first-class. The forwards need more weight to be effective.</p>
        <p>Warrington played a rather careless game and toyed with the opposition. Ike Taylor was really great at centre, and for once overshadowed Dickenson who was not looking for work. The trial of Jenkins at half was unsatisfactory, the Welshman appearing far from fit. Brooks scored two tries on the wing, but when he joined Lloyd at half it was soon seen that he could not be moved from the scrum. Lloyd again played a sound game., and silenced many critics by scoring three of the tries. He and Brooks got six out of the ten. •</p>
      </div>
