        <p>I or pointing out how poor we were, and how rich Mr. Lampson was; Of course, .that was true enough, although my mother is the Countess of Mounteastle." He let fall her hand. "And you ? " said he ,in a low voice. "I am Lady Alice Brydone." Lady Alice Brydone,whose name was associated with all that was good and charitable! Hallord started; he could not help it. "And you will be my wife ?" he asked, breath- lessly. Her answer was to slide her hand into his. Be drew her to him and kissed her again and again. have no fortune to offer VOU:." he whispered. "What does that matter ? You love me—l want nothing else." - LTIIE END.I Wycliffe P.S.A. The weekly meeting of this society was held on Sunday under the presidency of Mr. Beswick, and Mr. F. Adeock, of Manchester, gave the address upon. " The good Samaritan." Mr. Payton played an organ solo and the Chairmax . read the lesson. This being prize day over 300 books were distributed. The , secretary moved a vote of sympathy with one of the ; brethren (Mr. Hilson) ,on the death of his daughter, Mrs. Birehall, of Church-street ; also with Mr. William ; Ford on the death of hisson</p>
