        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT. DEBAfE ON THE EDUCATION [BILL • Although the subject down for debate—the Education Bill—at the meeting of our local Parliament on Thursday evening is one of the most vexed political questions of the day, the attendance of members was not up to the average. The vacant places were chiefly on the Opposition benches, the Licensing Bill protest meeting at the Parr Hall no doubt proving a more powerful attraction. Before the usual business of the House began a resolu- tion expressing regret at the death of the Duke of Devonshire was passed. MR. LLOYD-GEORGE AS ARBITRATOR. Mr. BARNES (Evesham), asked the Prime Minister if the Government could appoint Mr. Lloyd-George arbitrator in all industrial disputes, owing to the great confidence displayed towards him by all industrial communities. The PREMIER said the Government fully acknow- ledged the great work done by Mr. Lloyd-George in the settlement of industrial disputes but they considered that be did more good as a Cabinet Minister than he would do merely as a public. official. CATS t. Mr. G. H. W. STEEL (Northwich) asked if the Prime Minister would consider the suggestion of imposing a tax on cats, which would mean an increase of revenue of £3,000,000. This question, he said, was asked after a sleepless night owin_g to cats. (Laughter.) The PREMIER: It is not our intention to taX the harmless and necessary cat, but we should ireloome very heartily a proposal to heavily tax those persons who ask foolish questions. (Laughter.) THE EDUCATION QUESTION. Mr. S. W. JEFFERY (High Peak Division, Minister for Education) introduced the Education Bill in a powerful and well reasoned speech. He said he made no apology for departing from the lines of Mr. McKenna 's 1908 Bill. In so far as the 1902 Act abolished the dual system and aimed at the establishment of one class of schools it must be a measure welcomed by all true educationists. Mr. McKenna's proposed measure. aiming as it did at again erecting the dual system, must meet with the uncompromising opposition of those who aimed at a national system of education, nor could he believe it was a wise policy to divorce the granting of publics funds from public control. The one seemed to be the natural outcome of the other. The schools which con- tracted out would suffer very many disadvantages and disabilities. First of all they would be removed from all public control—in fact they might go back to the very worst form of private control—one iran manage- ment. There would also be a very material loss to the childrenattendingsuch schools. They would be debarred from taking part in many of the advantages which were now placed at their disposal by the Local Education Authority—attendance at manual instruction, the teach- ing of swimming which was now recognized as a great adjunct to our education, and participation in scholar- ships. There would also be the evils of inferior staffing forced on the managers by the non-obtaining of rate aid and there would be insufficient equipment. They might take it as quite true that this would be paid for by the same person who paid for it before—the teacher. He believed the teachers in contracting out schools would be unable to obtain the advantages which they bad been offered by the Teachers' Superannuation Fund. Turning to the Bill which it was his privilege to introduce, they found in it four chief points :—it established one system of schools throughout the land; it abolislied all religious instruction; it threw the whole -tharge upon the national exchequer, and it abolished all creed tests for teachers. (Applause.) It had been said that unless the Church had taken up education, it would have been neglected entirely—in fact the claim was made that the origin of education was religious,voluntary and denominational. (Opposition cheers.)A brief historical review would show that it was not so. In 1807 the House of Commons passed a Bill enabling the ratepayers to set, up a school in each parish and the House of Lords, as usual ever ready to oppose the democratic advance of the people—(hear, hear)—aivised by the Archbishop of Canterbury, threw it out. Mr. Jeffery quoted other instances in support of his argument and said that throughout the years the progress of true education had been retarded by sectarian differences. There was no religious difficulty whatever in the schools—(hear, hear),— which had too long been the battle ground of sectarian combatants with the result that the education of our people had lagged behind that of other nations. It was proposed to end that by a national system of educa- tion for secular instruction only. They would not deny the parents the right of instructing the children in the religion they desired, but no parent had a legal or moral right- to demand that special and religious teaching should be given at the public expense. (Government cheers.) The inculcation of doctrine and dogma required the most export teachers and both by training and selection the clergy were the most fitted to undertake that duty. The function of the teacher was not to send out little Churchmen or little Nonconformists but rather the making of good citizens who should be of service to the Empire. (Cheers.) With regard to other provisions of the Bill, he con- sidered that in an age when education was compulsory the payment of fees was quite beside the mark, nor would its abolition be of serious import to any except those parents who at great hardship and Much self-denial were called upon to pay them. They proposed to make the compulsory school age from 5 to 14 and not to allow, as was done at present, so many coaches and fours to run through the Act. The average duration of school life in England was the shortest on record in any nation. The Government further claimed that the teacher shnuld be submitted to tests only of character and ability. There were no tests imposed on other public officials. Why, should the teacher alone have to submit to religious and creed tests ? With regard to the raising of the money, Mr. Jeffery showed how the amounts available for education varied in each area under the present system. Where the assessable value was low there was the greatest primary school population and the burden on the ratepayers was very much greater than they could bear. The Govern- ment therefore proposed that the whole charge of nitional education should be thrown upon the State. (Cheers). Mr. G. WALKER (Ashton-under-Lyne, Home Secre- tary), seconded the adoption of the Bill. Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (Dublin University) moved that the Bill be read three months hence. They must congratulate the Minister for Education on bringing forward a real Education Bill in many respects—(cheers) .and he (Mr. Stansfield) agreed with him as to the expediency of bringing every school into the national system, the abolition of fees, the raising of the school age, and the charging of education upon the national exchequer. Where he seriously disagreed with the Bill was in the abolition of religious instruction. The Minister for Education had sought to show that the Church had opposed education but if that were so, it was a singular fact that the Church of England and the Church of Rome were practically the only two bodies who built schools. (Opposition and Nationalist cheers). To bundle out religion from the schools would be a fatal mistake, for religious and secular instructio should go together. They could not build up character on a secular basis. As to tests, he might be old- fashioned—(bear, hear)—but he thought tests ought to be applied to teachers. He sympathised with Noncon- formists in the handicap imposed by the stipulation that no teacher in Church and Roman Catholic schools could be appointed to a headship unless he subscribeil to a particular religious belief, and he suggested as a compromise that no bead teacher should be required as a condition of employment to subscribe to any creed but that assistants should be required to do so.He protested against the proposal that the denominational schools, valued at from £20.000,000 to £40,000,000, should be handed over to the authority without payment. Mr. VERNON (Maidstone) formally moved the rejec- tion of the mil. Mr. H. RIGBY (W. Belfast), said the Nationalists as Catholics did not want rent. They wanted Catholic schools for Catholic children, and that they would have. (Nationalist cheers.) He congratulated the Government on coming from behind the cloak of undenominationalism. The Bill meant that the whole of the day was to be spent in training the intellect of the child, whilst no time would be devoted to instilling into the child a knowledge of the source from which intellect sprang—the Great Creator. Secularism would not be final so far as Catholics were concerned, and they would fight it to the end. (Cheers.) He warned the Government not to convert the Nationalists from allies into enemies. . Mr. HALEY (Deptford), congratulated the Govern- ment on introducing a Bill which in its main provisions was satisfactory to the Socialists. They objected most strongly to sectarianism being a burden on the rates or the State. The religious element had hitherto hindered the efficient progress of education and should be removed. What was this religious education which hon. gentlemen desired so much ?—A voice from , the Opposition : "The foundation of England's I greatness " and cheers). The Bible was the I foundation of England's greatness but not very long I ago in the Parish Church, Canon Morley Stevenson said I it was not the Bible they wanted in the schools, but their distinctive religious creed. Mr. Haley argued for a free system of education from the elementary schools to the University. IMr. F. G. TAYLOR (Petersfleld), asked whether it was more necessary that a child should be taught swimming or religion? (Some Voices : Swimming). As to the I religion which the Opposition required to be taught, it included the existence of God and the Trinity, and I the true meaning of the life upon earth of Christ and what that life meant for all time to people who believed ,in Him. Be claimed that that was Bible teaching. Confiscatory as Mr. McKenna's Bill was, he claimed that this Bill was still more so. Mr.Taylorlwas arguing the point when The PREMIER said the debates of the House would not be helped by the imputation of immoral motives. The SPEAKER said he did not think the hon. member meant to imply immoral motives. He believed the Bill to be confiscatory and he had a right to his opinion. Mr. TAYLOR: I did not wish to imply anything in I the way of immoral motives, but only misconceived ideas of what is proper. (Laughter). , Mr. M. CUNNINGHAM (S. Donegal) argued strongly in favour of denominational teaching. " Goodnes knows " he said "We are bad enough now. What will happen • if you do away with religious instruction la the schools</p>
        <p>k" dread to think. The need for religion is greater now than ever it was." Mr. NIGHTINGALE (Normanton) in an impassioned speech repudiated the idea that Socialists were atheists and said their religion was to see that justice and righteousness were done to the oppressed and poor. He believed in Christianity as taught by Christ Who said "Love Thy neighbour as thyself" and" Do unto others as ye would they should do mato yon." He contended that the school age would be better from 7 to 18 years. At this stage Mr. W. MASON (Newcastle-on-Tyne) moved the adjournment of the debate and the House went into committee.</p>
