        <p>Warrington people heard with particular regret of the death of Major Arthur Griffiths, which was announced on Tuesday, for it is but a short time since he visited Warrington at the invitation of the War- rington Society to unveil the Howard Memorial in Bridge-street. The interesting speech which he 1 delivered on that occasion, embodying as it did a brief 1 biography of the great .prison reformer and the !expression of Major Griffiths' own views on the prison (system of our day, will not readily be forgotten. The iMajor had had a remarkably active and useful career. He served in the Crimea, and from 1864 to 1870 was captain and brigade-major at Gibraltar. He was afterwards deputy-governor of Chatham, Millbank, and Wormwood Scrubbs Prisons, and in 1878 became inspector of prisons,which position he held until 1896. He was cne of our greatest authorities on criminology, having devoted much attention to the life and modes of thought of the criminal classes, and he used the knowledge thus gained with great skill both in his works of fiction and in serious books. Major Griffiths was for some time editor for the " World " and of the " Fortnightly Review."</p>
